var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Core-API","page":"Core","text":"Pages = [\"api/core.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Core API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Core-Messages-and-Errors","page":"Core","text":"","title":"Core Messages and Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Header-and-Context-Helpers","page":"Core","text":"","title":"Header and Context Helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Body-Types-and-Streamed-Payloads","page":"Core","text":"","title":"Body Types and Streamed Payloads"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Cookies,-Forms,-and-Request-Bodies","page":"Core","text":"","title":"Cookies, Forms, and Request Bodies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#Proxy-Configuration","page":"Core","text":"","title":"Proxy Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/core/#HTTP/2-Configuration","page":"Core","text":"","title":"HTTP/2 Configuration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Request","page":"Core","text":"Request(method, target; headers=Headers(), body=nothing, trailers=Headers(),\n        host=nothing, content_length=-1, proto_major=1, proto_minor=1,\n        close=false, context=RequestContext(), copyheaders=true)\nRequest(method, target, headers[, body])\n\nAn HTTP request: what a server handler receives, and what the client sends.\n\nheaders, trailers: anything Headers accepts. They are copied unless copyheaders = false and they are Headers collections; the request then uses them directly, so do not touch them while the request is in use.\nbody: a String, a byte vector, an AbstractBody, or nothing.\nhost: the authority sent as the HTTP/1 Host header or HTTP/2 :authority.\ncontent_length: the body length in bytes. -1 computes it for a String, byte-vector, or empty body, and means unknown for other bodies.\nproto_major, proto_minor: the HTTP version.\nclose: close the connection after this request.\ncontext: the request's RequestContext.\n\nreq.context returns the context's application data as a Dict{Symbol,Any}; use get_request_context for the RequestContext itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Request"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Response","page":"Core","text":"Response(status, [body]; headers=Headers(), reason=\"\", trailers=Headers(),\n         content_length=-1, proto_major=1, proto_minor=1, close=false,\n         request=nothing)\nResponse(status, headers, body)\nResponse(body)\n\nAn HTTP response: what a server handler returns, and what the client returns.\n\nbody, passed by position or as a keyword, may be a String, a byte vector, an AbstractBody, or nothing. A String or byte vector is stored as is, so one Response can be returned for many requests. content_length defaults to the length of such a body. request is the request that produced the response. Response(body) uses status 200.\n\nA client response also has response.url, the final URL after redirects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Response"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Headers","page":"Core","text":"Headers()\nHeaders(items)\nHeaders(pairs...; kwargs...)\n\nAn ordered vector of String => String header pairs.\n\nHeader names match in any case (RFC 9110 §5.1): header, setheader, removeheader, haskey, and the other lookups ignore case, and each name keeps the spelling it was stored with. HTTP/1 sends names in that spelling; HTTP/2 sends them in lowercase. Headers read from the network are stored in canonical_header_key form, such as Content-Type, so a response looks the same over HTTP/1 and HTTP/2.\n\nitems may be a vector, dictionary, or tuple of pairs, and keyword arguments add more headers. The input is copied, and a header with the same name as the one before it is joined onto it, as in appendheader. Headers(n::Integer) returns an empty collection with room for n headers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Headers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.RequestContext","page":"Core","text":"RequestContext(; deadline_ns=0)\n\nCancellation, deadline, and application data for one request. Pass one to a client call with the context keyword; get_request_context returns the context of a request a server is handling.\n\ndeadline_ns is an absolute time_ns() value, or 0 for no deadline. A client request still running at the deadline throws TimeoutError, and cancel! makes it throw CanceledError.\n\nApplication data is stored under Symbol keys:\n\nctx = HTTP.RequestContext()\nctx[:request_id] = \"abc\"\nget(ctx, :request_id, nothing)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RequestContext"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.HTTPError","page":"Core","text":"HTTPError\n\nAbstract supertype for HTTP-specific exceptions raised by HTTP.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.HTTPError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.ParseError","page":"Core","text":"ParseError\n\nRaised when byte-level HTTP syntax cannot be parsed. This is used for malformed request/status lines, invalid header syntax, truncated framed bodies, and other wire-format failures where the peer did not send valid HTTP.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ParseError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.ProtocolError","page":"Core","text":"ProtocolError\n\nRaised when the bytes are syntactically valid but violate higher-level HTTP rules. Examples include mismatched Content-Length values, impossible frame ordering, or unsupported control-flow states in the client/server stacks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ProtocolError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.CanceledError","page":"Core","text":"CanceledError\n\nRaised when request processing is canceled explicitly through RequestContext. Unlike ParseError and ProtocolError, this usually reflects local control flow rather than a bad peer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.CanceledError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.TimeoutError","page":"Core","text":"TimeoutError\n\nRaised when a client timeout or deadline expires.\n\nFields:\n\noperation::String: \"connect\" or \"tls_handshake\" when connect_timeout expired, \"websocket handshake\" for a WebSocket handshake, and \"request\" for the other timeouts and for deadlines.\ntimeout_ns::Int64: the timeout that expired, in nanoseconds, or 0 when it is not known.\nelapsed_ns::Int64: the time spent before it expired, in nanoseconds, or 0 when it is not known.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.TimeoutError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.HTTPTimeoutError","page":"Core","text":"HTTPTimeoutError\n\nAnother name for TimeoutError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.HTTPTimeoutError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.StatusError","page":"Core","text":"StatusError\n\nRaised by request and the verb helpers for a response with status 300 or above that is not a redirect, unless status_exception = false.\n\nFields:\n\nstatus::Int16: the response status.\nresponse::Response: the response, including its body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.StatusError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.TooManyRedirectsError","page":"Core","text":"TooManyRedirectsError\n\nRaised when a request reaches its redirect limit and gets another redirect. limit is the limit and response is the last redirect response.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.TooManyRedirectsError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.ConnectError","page":"Core","text":"ConnectError(address, cause)\n\nRaised when the client cannot connect to address (host:port). cause is the underlying exception.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ConnectError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.DNSError","page":"Core","text":"DNSError(hostname, cause)\n\nRaised when the client cannot resolve hostname. cause is the underlying exception.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.DNSError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.TLSHandshakeError","page":"Core","text":"TLSHandshakeError(cause)\n\nRaised when a TLS handshake fails (other than a handshake timeout, which surfaces as TimeoutError with operation = \"tls_handshake\"). TLS failures after connection setup surface as TLSTransportError instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.TLSHandshakeError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.TLSTransportError","page":"Core","text":"TLSTransportError(cause)\n\nRaised when TLS-level I/O fails on an established connection during a request — for example a connection reset or a truncated TLS stream while writing the request or reading the response. cause is the underlying Reseau.TLS.TLSError. TLS failures during connection setup surface as TLSHandshakeError (or TimeoutError for handshake timeouts) instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.TLSTransportError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.AddressInUseError","page":"Core","text":"AddressInUseError(address)\n\nRaised when a server cannot bind because the requested address is already in use. address records the bind target (host:port).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.AddressInUseError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.canonical_header_key","page":"Core","text":"canonical_header_key(key) -> String\n\nReturn the header name key in canonical form, such as Content-Type: the first letter and every letter after a - are uppercase, and the other ASCII letters are lowercase. key is not checked for validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.canonical_header_key"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.header","page":"Core","text":"header(headers, key, default=\"\")\nheader(message, key, default=\"\")\n\nReturn the first value of header key, or default if there is none. key matches in any case. message is a Request or Response.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.header"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.headers","page":"Core","text":"headers(headers, key) -> Vector{String}\nheaders(message, key) -> Vector{String}\n\nReturn every value of header key, in order, or String[] if there is none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.headers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.hasheader","page":"Core","text":"hasheader(headers, key) -> Bool\nhasheader(message, key) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when header key is present and its first value is not empty.\n\n\n\n\n\nhasheader(headers, key, value) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when any stored header value for key matches value case-insensitively.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.hasheader"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.headercontains","page":"Core","text":"headercontains(headers, key, token) -> Bool\nheadercontains(message, key, token) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when a value of header key, read as a comma-separated list, contains token. Both the name and the token match in any case. Use it for list-valued headers such as Connection and Transfer-Encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.headercontains"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.setheader","page":"Core","text":"setheader(headers, key => value) -> headers\nsetheader(headers, key, value) -> headers\nsetheader(message, key => value) -> message\n\nReplace every value of header key with value. The header keeps the position of its first occurrence and takes the spelling of key; a new header goes at the end. setheader! is the same function.\n\n\n\n\n\nsetheader(stream, key, value) -> nothing\nsetheader(stream, key => value) -> nothing\n\nSet a header of the response on a server stream. Call it before the first write; after that it throws ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.setheader"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.setheader!","page":"Core","text":"setheader!(headers, key => value) -> headers\nsetheader!(headers, key, value) -> headers\nsetheader!(message, key => value) -> message\n\nThe same function as setheader.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.setheader!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.defaultheader!","page":"Core","text":"defaultheader!(headers, key => value) -> headers\ndefaultheader!(message, key => value) -> message\n\nAdd key => value unless header key is already present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.defaultheader!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.appendheader","page":"Core","text":"appendheader(headers, key => value) -> headers\nappendheader(headers, key, value) -> headers\nappendheader(message, key => value) -> message\n\nAdd a header value. When the last header has the same name, in any case, the value is joined onto it instead: with \"; \" for Cookie (RFC 6265 §5.4) and with \",\" for other names (RFC 9110 §5.3). Set-Cookie values always become separate headers. appendheader! is the same function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.appendheader"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.appendheader!","page":"Core","text":"appendheader!(headers, key => value) -> headers\nappendheader!(headers, key, value) -> headers\nappendheader!(message, key => value) -> message\n\nThe same function as appendheader.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.appendheader!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.removeheader","page":"Core","text":"removeheader(headers, key) -> headers\nremoveheader(message, key) -> message\n\nRemove every value of header key. removeheader! is the same function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.removeheader"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.removeheader!","page":"Core","text":"removeheader!(headers, key) -> headers\nremoveheader!(message, key) -> message\n\nThe same function as removeheader.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.removeheader!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.mkheaders","page":"Core","text":"mkheaders(headers_input) -> Headers\n\nConvert headers_input to Headers. It may be nothing, a pair or 2-tuple, a dictionary, or an iterable of pairs or 2-tuples. A Headers is returned as is, without a copy. Each value, including each element of a vector value, is added with appendheader, which joins it onto the previous header when the names match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.mkheaders"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.get_request_context","page":"Core","text":"get_request_context(request) -> RequestContext\n\nReturn the RequestContext of request, which holds its cancellation state and deadline. request.context returns only the context's application data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.get_request_context"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.set_deadline!","page":"Core","text":"set_deadline!(ctx, deadline_ns) -> ctx\n\nSet the deadline of ctx to deadline_ns, an absolute time_ns() value. 0 removes the deadline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.set_deadline!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.cancel!","page":"Core","text":"cancel!(ctx; message=\"request canceled\") -> ctx\n\nCancel the request that uses ctx. Its current read or write stops, and the request throws a CanceledError carrying message. cancel! does not throw itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.cancel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.canceled","page":"Core","text":"canceled(ctx) -> Bool\n\nReturn true once cancel! has been called on ctx.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.canceled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.expired","page":"Core","text":"expired(ctx, now_ns=time_ns()) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when ctx has a deadline and now_ns has reached it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.expired"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.AbstractBody","page":"Core","text":"AbstractBody\n\nSupertype of request and response bodies that are read as a stream.\n\nA subtype implements body_read!, body_close!, and body_closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.AbstractBody"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.CallbackBody","page":"Core","text":"CallbackBody(read_cb, close_cb)\n\nA body produced by a function. read_cb(dst::Vector{UInt8}) fills the start of dst and returns the number of bytes written, or 0 at the end of the body. close_cb() runs once, when the body is closed. A CallbackBody can be read only once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.CallbackBody"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/core/#HTTP.nobody","page":"Core","text":"An empty Vector{UInt8} to pass as a body that has no bytes. It is shared, so do not modify it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.nobody"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.body_read!","page":"Core","text":"body_read!(body, dst) -> Int\n\nRead up to length(dst) bytes of body into dst and return how many were read, or 0 at the end of the body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.body_read!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.body_close!","page":"Core","text":"body_close!(body)\n\nClose body and release what it holds. Closing a closed body does nothing, so it is safe to call in a finally block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.body_close!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.body_closed","page":"Core","text":"body_closed(body) -> Bool\n\nReturn true once body_close! has closed body. Reading a body to the end does not close it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.body_closed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.read_request","page":"Core","text":"read_request(io; max_line_bytes=64*1024, max_header_bytes=1024*1024) -> Request\n\nRead one HTTP/1 request head from io and return it as a Request whose body reads the rest of the request from io. max_line_bytes limits each line and max_header_bytes the whole head. Throws ParseError or ProtocolError for an invalid request.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.read_request"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.write_request!","page":"Core","text":"write_request!(io, request)\n\nWrite request to io in HTTP/1.1 format, with its body.\n\nThe Host header comes first (RFC 9110 §7.2), taken from the request's Host header or else from request.host. A body of known length is sent with Content-Length, and any other body with chunked encoding, the only framing that carries trailers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.write_request!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.write_response!","page":"Core","text":"write_response!(io, response, request=response.request)\n\nWrite response to io in HTTP/1.1 format, with its body. No body is written for 1xx, 204, and 304 responses, or when request is a HEAD request.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.write_response!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.trailers","page":"Core","text":"trailers(body)\n\nReturn a copy of the trailers that ended a chunked body, or empty Headers for other bodies. Read the body to the end first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.trailers"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.Cookie","page":"Core","text":"Cookie(name, value; kwargs...)\n\nA cookie, as sent in a request's Cookie header or a response's Set-Cookie header. Set its other fields, such as path, domain, expires, maxage, secure, httponly, and samesite (:lax, :strict, or :none), with keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.Cookie"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.CookieJar","page":"Core","text":"CookieJar()\nCookieJar(entries::Dict{String, Dict{String, Cookie}})\n\nAn in-memory cookie store for a Client or the cookiejar keyword of a request. It stores cookies from responses and picks the ones to send by their domain, path, expiry, and Secure flag.\n\njar.entries holds the cookies, keyed by host. To keep cookies across sessions, save jar.entries and later pass it to CookieJar(entries), which uses the Dict without copying it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.CookieJar"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.cookies","page":"Core","text":"cookies(request_or_response) -> Vector{Cookie}\n\nReturn the cookies in a request's Cookie header or a response's Set-Cookie headers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.cookies"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.stringify","page":"Core","text":"stringify(cookie, isrequest=true) -> String\nstringify(prefix, cookies, isrequest=true) -> String\n\nFormat cookies as header text. With isrequest = true the result is name=value, as in a Cookie header; with false it also has the attributes of a Set-Cookie header, such as Path, Domain, and HttpOnly. The second form appends cookies to the header text prefix, separated by \"; \".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.stringify"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.getcookies!","page":"Core","text":"getcookies!(jar, scheme, host, path[, now]) -> Vector{Cookie}\n\nReturn the cookies from jar to send with a request to scheme://host/path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.getcookies!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.setcookies!","page":"Core","text":"setcookies!(jar, scheme, host, path, headers) -> nothing\n\nStore the Set-Cookie headers of a response from scheme://host/path in jar.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.setcookies!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Cookies.addcookie!","page":"Core","text":"addcookie!(message, cookie) -> message\n\nAdd cookie to the Cookie header of a Request, or as a new Set-Cookie header of a Response.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Cookies.addcookie!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Form","page":"Core","text":"Form(parts; boundary, type=:formdata) <: IO\n\nA multipart request body. parts is a collection of name => value pairs. An IO value is streamed (an open file is sent with its file name, and a Multipart with its own name and content type); any other value is sent as string(value). type = :mixed builds a multipart/mixed body instead of multipart/form-data. A request with a Form body gets the matching Content-Type header from content_type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Form"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Multipart","page":"Core","text":"Multipart(filename, data, contenttype=\"\", contenttransferencoding=\"\", name=\"\") <: IO\n\nOne part of a multipart body, with its content in the IO data. filename may be nothing, and an empty contenttype is detected from the data when the part is sent. The parse_multipart functions return Multiparts, and Form accepts them as values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Multipart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.Batch","page":"Core","text":"Batch(parts; boundary) -> Form\n\nBuild a multipart/mixed body, the format of batch APIs such as OData $batch. parts is a vector of Multiparts or a collection of name => value pairs, as for Form with type = :mixed.\n\nparts = [\n    HTTP.Multipart(nothing, IOBuffer(\"GET /users/1 HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\\r\\n\"), \"application/http\"),\n    HTTP.Multipart(nothing, IOBuffer(\"GET /users/2 HTTP/1.1\\r\\n\\r\\n\"), \"application/http\"),\n]\nresponse = HTTP.post(url, [], HTTP.Batch(parts))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.content_type","page":"Core","text":"content_type(form) -> String\n\nReturn the Content-Type header value for form: multipart/form-data or multipart/mixed, with the boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.content_type"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.parse_multipart_form","page":"Core","text":"parse_multipart_form(content_type_header, body; max_parts=1000) -> Union{Vector{Multipart}, Nothing}\nparse_multipart_form(message; max_parts=1000) -> Union{Vector{Multipart}, Nothing}\n\nParse a multipart/form-data body, using the boundary in content_type_header. The message form reads the Content-Type header and body of a Request or Response, such as the request in a serve! handler.\n\nReturns nothing when the content type is not multipart/form-data or there is no body. Each Multipart has the part's name, filename (or nothing), contenttype, and its content as the IO data. A body with more than max_parts parts throws an error; max_parts = 0 removes the limit.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> body = Vector{UInt8}(\n           \"--boundary\\r\\n\" *\n           \"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\\\"greeting\\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n\" *\n           \"hello\\r\\n\" *\n           \"--boundary\\r\\n\" *\n           \"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\\\"file\\\"; filename=\\\"a.txt\\\"\\r\\n\" *\n           \"Content-Type: text/plain\\r\\n\\r\\n\" *\n           \"file contents\\r\\n\" *\n           \"--boundary--\\r\\n\");\n\njulia> parts = HTTP.parse_multipart_form(\"multipart/form-data; boundary=boundary\", body);\n\njulia> length(parts)\n2\n\njulia> parts[1].name, String(read(parts[1].data))\n(\"greeting\", \"hello\")\n\njulia> parts[2].name, parts[2].filename, parts[2].contenttype\n(\"file\", \"a.txt\", \"text/plain\")\n\njulia> String(read(parts[2].data))\n\"file contents\"\n\nInside a server handler, pass the request directly:\n\nHTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do request\n    parts = HTTP.parse_multipart_form(request)\n    parts === nothing && return HTTP.Response(415, \"expected multipart/form-data\")\n    return HTTP.Response(200, \"received $(length(parts)) part(s)\")\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.parse_multipart_form"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.parse_multipart","page":"Core","text":"parse_multipart(content_type_header, body, required_type=nothing; max_parts=1000) -> Union{Vector{Multipart}, Nothing}\nparse_multipart(message, required_type=nothing; max_parts=1000) -> Union{Vector{Multipart}, Nothing}\n\nParse a multipart body of any subtype, such as multipart/form-data or multipart/mixed, using the boundary in content_type_header. The message form reads the header and body of a Request or Response.\n\nrequired_type (:formdata or :mixed) accepts only that subtype. Returns nothing when the content type does not match or there is no body. A body with more than max_parts parts throws an error; max_parts = 0 removes the limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.parse_multipart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.parse_multipart_mixed","page":"Core","text":"parse_multipart_mixed(content_type_header, body; max_parts=1000) -> Union{Vector{Multipart}, Nothing}\nparse_multipart_mixed(message; max_parts=1000) -> Union{Vector{Multipart}, Nothing}\n\nParse a multipart/mixed body; see parse_multipart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.parse_multipart_mixed"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.ProxyConfig","page":"Core","text":"ProxyConfig(url; no_proxy=nothing)\nProxyConfig(; http=nothing, https=nothing, all=nothing, no_proxy=nothing, env=false)\n\nThe proxy settings of a Transport or a request. ProxyConfig() connects directly.\n\nhttp is the proxy for http:// URLs, https the proxy for https:// URLs, and all the proxy for either when the other is not set; ProxyConfig(url) sets all. no_proxy is a NoProxy, or anything it accepts, naming hosts to reach directly. With env = true, settings that are not given come from the HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, and NO_PROXY environment variables, in upper or lower case.\n\nProxy URLs may use http://, socks5://, or socks5h://; with either SOCKS scheme, the proxy resolves host names. An https:// proxy URL is accepted, but a request through it throws ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ProxyConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.ProxyURL","page":"Core","text":"ProxyURL(url; no_proxy=nothing) -> ProxyConfig\n\nReturn a ProxyConfig that sends every request through the proxy at url, except requests to the hosts in no_proxy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ProxyURL"},{"category":"function","location":"api/core/#HTTP.ProxyFromEnvironment","page":"Core","text":"ProxyFromEnvironment() -> ProxyConfig\n\nReturn a ProxyConfig read from the HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, and NO_PROXY environment variables, in upper or lower case. The variables are read once, when it is called. Requests to localhost and loopback addresses never use these proxies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ProxyFromEnvironment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.NoProxy","page":"Core","text":"NoProxy(spec)\nNoProxy()\n\nThe hosts that a ProxyConfig reaches without its proxy, in the format of the NO_PROXY environment variable. spec is a comma-separated string or a vector of entries: host names, domain suffixes, IP addresses, CIDR ranges, or any of these with a :port. \"*\" matches every host. localhost and loopback addresses always match.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.NoProxy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/core/#HTTP.HTTP2Settings","page":"Core","text":"HTTP2Settings(; initial_window_size=65535, connection_window_size=65535)\n\nHTTP/2 receive windows, passed as http2_settings to a Client or to serve! and listen!.\n\ninitial_window_size: the receive window of each stream, sent as SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE (RFC 9113 §6.5.2). Values from 1 to 2^31 - 1 are allowed, so it can also be lowered to slow down senders.\nconnection_window_size: the receive window shared by all streams on a connection, from 65535 to 2^31 - 1.\n\nBoth default to 65535, the protocol default. One stream can move at most about window / round-trip time bytes per second, so raise them for large transfers over links with high latency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.HTTP2Settings"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/protocols/#Protocols-Guide","page":"Protocols","text":"This page covers WebSockets and HTTP/2. HTTP/2 needs no separate API: the client and server functions from the other guides use it when both sides support it.","title":"Protocols Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/protocols/#WebSockets","page":"Protocols","text":"The WebSocket API lives in the HTTP.WebSockets submodule. using HTTP also exports the name WebSockets; these docs write HTTP.WebSockets so that another package's WebSockets cannot shadow it.\n\nHTTP.WebSockets.listen! starts a server that calls your function with each new connection. HTTP.WebSockets.open connects to a ws:// or wss:// URL:\n\nusing HTTP\n\nserver = HTTP.WebSockets.listen!(\"127.0.0.1\", 0) do ws\n    for msg in ws\n        HTTP.WebSockets.send(ws, uppercase(msg))\n    end\nend\n\nurl = \"ws://\" * HTTP.WebSockets.server_addr(server)\nreply = HTTP.WebSockets.open(url) do ws\n    HTTP.WebSockets.send(ws, \"hello\")\n    HTTP.WebSockets.receive(ws)\nend\nHTTP.WebSockets.forceclose(server)\nreply  # \"HELLO\"\n\nIterating over a WebSocket yields each message until the peer closes the connection. receive returns a String for a text message and a Vector{UInt8} for a binary one. The do block form of open returns the block's value and closes the connection when the block ends.\n\nTo serve WebSockets and ordinary HTTP routes from one server, upgrade requests by hand inside an HTTP.listen! handler; see HTTP.WebSockets.upgrade.\n\nHTTP.WebSockets.open also accepts an IO that is already connected, such as a TCPSocket or a TLS stream, instead of a URL. Pass the request target and host as keywords. open never closes that IO:\n\nws = HTTP.WebSockets.open(io; target = \"/echo\", host = \"example.com\")","title":"WebSockets"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/protocols/#Limits-and-timeouts","page":"Protocols","text":"maxframesize limits the size of one incoming frame, and maxfragmentation the number of frames in one message. They default to 16 MiB and 1024 on clients and servers; pass larger values if your protocol needs them. A peer that goes over a limit gets a close frame with status 1009.\n\nHTTP.WebSockets.open with a URL accepts the client timeouts connect_timeout, request_timeout, response_header_timeout, read_idle_timeout, and write_idle_timeout. read_idle_timeout keeps applying after the handshake: if nothing arrives for that long, the connection closes and receive throws a WebSocketError.","title":"Limits and timeouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/protocols/#Compression","page":"Protocols","text":"Pass compress = true to use the permessage-deflate extension (RFC 7692), which compresses each message. Both sides must ask for it; otherwise messages are sent uncompressed. HTTP.WebSockets.listen!, HTTP.WebSockets.upgrade, and HTTP.WebSockets.open accept the keyword:\n\nserver = HTTP.WebSockets.listen!(\"127.0.0.1\", 0; compress = true) do ws\n    for msg in ws\n        HTTP.WebSockets.send(ws, msg)\n    end\nend\n\nHTTP.WebSockets.open(\"ws://\" * HTTP.WebSockets.server_addr(server); compress = true) do ws\n    HTTP.WebSockets.send(ws, repeat(\"compress me \", 1000))\n    HTTP.WebSockets.receive(ws)\nend\n\nCompression helps most with large, repetitive text such as JSON. The maxframesize limit applies to the decompressed message.","title":"Compression"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/protocols/#HTTP/2","page":"Protocols","text":"The client asks for HTTP/2 when it connects to an https:// URL, and uses it when the server agrees. Pass prefer_http2 = false to HTTP.Client, or protocol = :h1 to a single request, to use HTTP/1.1 instead. protocol = :h2 with an http:// URL sends cleartext HTTP/2 without first asking the server, which works with servers that accept it, including HTTP.jl's.\n\nh1_only = HTTP.Client(prefer_http2 = false)\nresp = HTTP.get(h1_only, \"https://example.com\")\nclose(h1_only)\n\nServers started with HTTP.serve! or HTTP.listen! speak HTTP/2 too. Over TLS, the client and server agree on HTTP/2 during the TLS handshake, which is what browsers require. On a plain listener, a client that opens with the HTTP/2 connection preface gets HTTP/2; pass allow_h2c = false to turn that off. Requests with an Upgrade: h2c header are answered with HTTP/1.1.","title":"HTTP/2"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/protocols/#Flow-control-windows","page":"Protocols","text":"HTTP/2 flow control limits how many bytes can be in flight on a stream before the receiver acknowledges them. With the protocol default window of 65,535 bytes, one stream can move at most about window / round-trip time per second, which slows large transfers on high-latency links. Pass an HTTP.HTTP2Settings as http2_settings to raise the receive windows. The server's setting affects uploads and the client's affects downloads:\n\nsettings = HTTP.HTTP2Settings(\n    initial_window_size = 1 << 20,     # 1 MiB per stream\n    connection_window_size = 1 << 21,  # 2 MiB per connection\n)\n\nclient = HTTP.Client(http2_settings = settings)\nserver = HTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080; http2_settings = settings) do req\n    return HTTP.Response(200; body = \"ok\")\nend\n\nBoth windows default to 65,535 bytes.","title":"Flow-control windows"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Server-Guide","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.serve! calls your function with each HTTP.Request and sends the HTTP.Response it returns. HTTP.listen! calls your function with an HTTP.Stream instead, so it can read the request body and write the response in pieces. Both return an HTTP.Server right away; HTTP.serve and HTTP.listen take the same arguments and block until the server closes.","title":"Server Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Request-Handlers","page":"Server","text":"using HTTP\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do req\n    return HTTP.Response(200; headers = [\"Content-Type\" => \"text/plain\"], body = \"handled \" * req.target)\nend\n\nresp = HTTP.get(\"http://127.0.0.1:8080/health\")\nString(resp.body)  # \"handled /health\"\nHTTP.forceclose(server)\n\nA handler that throws sends the client a 500 Internal Server Error.","title":"Request Handlers"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Reading-the-request","page":"Server","text":"req.method and req.target hold the request line, and HTTP.header(req, \"Content-Type\") reads a header. String(req.body) returns the body as text.\n\nreq.target holds the path and the query string. Split them with HTTP.URI and decode the query with HTTP.queryparams, which returns a Dict, or HTTP.queryparampairs, which keeps the order and repeated keys. A form body (application/x-www-form-urlencoded) uses the same encoding, so HTTP.queryparams decodes it too:\n\nHTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do req\n    query = HTTP.queryparams(HTTP.URI(req.target).query)\n    form = HTTP.queryparams(String(req.body))\n    return HTTP.Response(200; body = \"page $(get(query, \"page\", \"1\")), user $(get(form, \"user\", \"?\"))\")\nend\n\nUse HTTP.parse_multipart_form for a multipart/form-data body.\n\nserve! reads the whole request body into memory before it calls the handler. max_body_bytes limits the size, 64 MiB by default; a larger body gets 413 Content Too Large. Pass max_body_bytes = 0 for no limit, or use a stream handler to read large bodies in pieces.","title":"Reading the request"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Reusing-responses","page":"Server","text":"A Response with a String or Vector{UInt8} body is not used up when it is sent, so a handler can return the same response object every time:\n\nconst HEALTH = HTTP.Response(200; headers = [\"Content-Type\" => \"text/plain\"], body = \"ok\")\nhandler(req) = HEALTH\n\nA streamed body, such as an HTTP.CallbackBody, can be sent only once. Sending it again gives the client a 500 Internal Server Error.","title":"Reusing responses"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Stream-Handlers","page":"Server","text":"A stream handler reads the request and writes the response itself:\n\nserver = HTTP.listen!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do stream\n    req = HTTP.startread(stream)  # the request line and headers\n    body = read(stream)           # the request body\n    HTTP.setstatus(stream, 200)\n    HTTP.setheader(stream, \"Content-Type\" => \"text/plain\")\n    write(stream, \"got $(length(body)) bytes for $(req.target)\")\nend\n\nSet the status and headers before the first write. A response without a Content-Length header is sent with chunked encoding, and HTTP.addtrailer adds trailers to it. HTTP.streamhandler(f) turns a request handler f into a stream handler.","title":"Stream Handlers"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Fixed-length-responses","page":"Server","text":"A stream handler that sets Content-Length on an HTTP/1 response and writes without calling HTTP.startwrite has its body held in memory until it returns or calls closewrite. Until then nothing is sent, so if the handler throws or writes the wrong number of bytes, the client gets an error response instead.\n\nTo stream a large body, call HTTP.startwrite first. The server still holds the first 4 KiB, so a small response goes out in one write with its head. Once the body passes 4 KiB, the head and the held bytes are sent, and each later write goes straight to the connection:\n\nHTTP.listen!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do stream\n    HTTP.setheader(stream, \"Content-Length\" => string(filesize(path)))\n    HTTP.startwrite(stream)\n    open(io -> write(stream, io), path)\nend\n\nOnce the head is sent, a failure can only close the connection. Either way, writing more than Content-Length bytes throws. HTTP.streamhandler streams a response body this way when it is not already in memory, such as a fileserver file.","title":"Fixed-length responses"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Interactive-Thread-Pool","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.jl runs its server tasks, including your handlers, on Julia's :interactive thread pool. That keeps the server answering requests, health checks included, while compute-heavy tasks keep the :default pool busy.\n\nStart a production server with at least one interactive thread. This command starts four default threads and one interactive thread:\n\njulia --threads=4,1 --project=. server.jl\n\nJULIA_NUM_THREADS=4,1 does the same. Without an interactive thread, Julia runs these tasks on the default pool, where tasks that never yield can stall the server.\n\nDo not run long computations directly in a handler: they hold an interactive thread. Run them on the default pool and wait for the result:\n\nresult = fetch(Threads.@spawn :default expensive_work())","title":"Interactive Thread Pool"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Server-Lifecycle","page":"Server","text":"Keep the Server that serve! or listen! returns:\n\nHTTP.port(server) returns the bound port, which is useful with port 0.\nwait(server) blocks until the server stops accepting connections.\nclose(server) stops accepting connections and waits for the requests in progress to finish.\nHTTP.forceclose(server) stops at once and closes every connection.","title":"Server Lifecycle"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Timeouts","page":"Server","text":"All server timeouts are off by default. Each keyword takes seconds, and a variant ending in _ns, such as read_timeout_ns, takes nanoseconds:\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(\n    handler,\n    \"127.0.0.1\",\n    8080;\n    read_header_timeout = 5,\n    read_timeout = 30,\n    write_timeout = 30,\n    idle_timeout = 120,\n)\n\nFor HTTP/1 connections, read_header_timeout limits reading the request line and headers. After the headers, read_timeout starts a fresh limit for reading the body. Between requests on a kept-alive connection, idle_timeout applies until the next request starts to arrive; the header limit starts then. When the header or idle setting is 0, it falls back to read_timeout.\n\nWhen a header or body read times out, the server answers 408 Request Timeout and closes the connection. An idle timeout closes the connection without a response.","title":"Timeouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#TLS","page":"Server","text":"To serve HTTPS, pass serve! or listen! a TLS listener from Reseau. List \"h2\" in alpn_protocols to offer HTTP/2 to clients:\n\nusing HTTP, Reseau\n\nconfig = Reseau.TLS.Config(;\n    cert_file = \"cert.pem\",\n    key_file = \"key.pem\",\n    alpn_protocols = [\"h2\", \"http/1.1\"],\n)\nserver = HTTP.serve!(handler, Reseau.TLS.listen(\"tcp\", \"127.0.0.1:8443\", config))\n\nSee the Protocols guide for HTTP/2 on plain connections.","title":"TLS"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Routing-and-Middleware","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.Router matches a request's method and path against registered routes:\n\nusing HTTP\n\nrouter = HTTP.Router()\n\nHTTP.register!(router, \"GET\", \"/users/{id}\") do req\n    id = HTTP.getparam(req, \"id\")\n    return HTTP.Response(200; body = \"user \" * id)\nend\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(router, \"127.0.0.1\", 8080)\n\nRead the matched route and its path variables with HTTP.getroute, HTTP.getparams, and HTTP.getparam. A path that matches no route gets 404 Not Found, and a path that matches with another method gets 405 Method Not Allowed.\n\nMiddleware is a function that takes a handler and returns a new handler. The third argument of Router wraps every route registered on it. This router gives each handler 5 seconds:\n\ntimeout = HTTP.Handlers.handlertimeout(5.0; status = 503)\nrouter = HTTP.Router(\n    req -> HTTP.Response(404),\n    req -> HTTP.Response(405),\n    timeout,\n)","title":"Routing and Middleware"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Request-logging","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.Handlers.logging_middleware wraps a handler and logs one record per request through Julia's logging system:\n\nusing HTTP, Logging\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(HTTP.Handlers.logging_middleware(router), \"127.0.0.1\", 8080)\n\nWith the default ConsoleLogger, each request prints a record like:\n\n┌ Info: GET /users/42 200 0.412ms\n│   method = \"GET\"\n│   target = \"/users/42\"\n│   status = 200\n│   elapsed_ms = 0.412\n└   length = 7\n\nlength is the response body length when it is known without reading the body. A handler that throws is logged at Logging.Error with the exception, which is then rethrown, so the client still gets an error response. Pass level to log successful requests at another level, and logger to send the records to a specific logger:\n\naccess_log = HTTP.Handlers.logging_middleware(router; level = Logging.Debug)\n\nThe middleware also wraps stream handlers; their records add the client's peer address. Every record is in the :access log group, so it is easy to filter or route with a package such as LoggingExtras.jl.","title":"Request logging"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Static-Files","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.fileserver(root) returns a request handler that serves the files under the directory root:\n\nusing HTTP\n\nhandler = HTTP.fileserver(\"public\"; spa_fallback = \"index.html\")\nserver = HTTP.serve!(handler, \"127.0.0.1\", 8080)\n\nIt serves index.html for a directory and redirects to the canonical path of a file or directory. spa_fallback serves the given file for a missing path that does not look like a file name, as a single-page app needs. It answers If-Modified-Since and single Range requests, and also If-None-Match when you pass etag.\n\nHTTP.servefile serves one file for a request, and HTTP.servecontent serves bytes or a seekable IO that you already have, with the same conditional and range handling. For a string, pass codeunits(str).","title":"Static Files"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#Server-Sent-Events","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.sse_stream builds a text/event-stream response. Write HTTP.SSEEvent values in its do block, which runs on its own task; the stream ends when the block returns:\n\nusing HTTP\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do req\n    return HTTP.sse_stream(200) do stream\n        write(stream, HTTP.SSEEvent(\"ready\"; event = \"status\", id = \"1\"))\n    end\nend\n\nA client reads the events with the sse_callback keyword of HTTP.request.","title":"Server-Sent Events"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/server/#HTTP/2","page":"Server","text":"serve! and listen! speak HTTP/2 to clients that ask for it, through TLS (see TLS) or cleartext prior knowledge. The Protocols guide covers the details and the http2_settings keyword.","title":"HTTP/2"},{"category":"section","location":"api/reference/#API-Reference","page":"Overview","text":"These pages hold the docstrings of HTTP.jl's public API. The guides explain how the pieces fit together.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/reference/#Module","page":"Overview","text":"","title":"Module"},{"category":"section","location":"api/reference/#Reference-Map","page":"Overview","text":"Core API: requests, responses, headers, errors, bodies, cookies, forms, proxies, and HTTP/2 settings.\nClient API: Client, Transport, the request functions, streaming, retries, and trace events.\nServer API: Server, Stream, routing, middleware, static files, and server-sent events.\nWebSockets API: WebSocket clients, servers, and messages.","title":"Reference Map"},{"category":"module","location":"api/reference/#HTTP.HTTP","page":"Overview","text":"HTTP\n\nAn HTTP client and server library for HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, with WebSockets and server-sent events. Reseau provides the TCP, DNS, and TLS layers.\n\nrequest, and get, post, put, patch, delete, head, options, and query, send a request and return the response.\nopen streams a request and its response.\nserve! and serve run a server whose handler maps a Request to a Response.\nlisten! and listen run a server whose handler reads and writes a Stream.\nWebSockets has the WebSocket client and server.\nSSEEvent and sse_stream send server-sent events.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.HTTP"},{"category":"section","location":"api/server/#Server-API","page":"Server","text":"Pages = [\"api/server.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Server API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/server/#Server-Lifecycle-and-Request-Handlers","page":"Server","text":"","title":"Server Lifecycle and Request Handlers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/server/#Routing-and-Middleware","page":"Server","text":"The router and middleware live in HTTP.Handlers. Except for handlertimeout and logging_middleware, the names below are also available from HTTP directly, as in HTTP.Router and HTTP.register!.","title":"Routing and Middleware"},{"category":"section","location":"api/server/#Server-Sent-Events","page":"Server","text":"","title":"Server-Sent Events"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Server","page":"Server","text":"Server(; network=\"tcp\", address=\"127.0.0.1:0\", handler, stream=false, ...)\n\nAn HTTP server, returned by serve! and listen!. port returns its port. close(server) stops accepting connections and waits for the requests in progress to finish, forceclose closes every connection at once, and wait(server) returns when the server stops accepting connections.\n\nServer tasks, including handlers, run on Julia's :interactive thread pool. Start Julia with at least one interactive thread, such as --threads=4,1, so that tasks on the default pool that never yield cannot stall the server. Without an interactive thread, these tasks run on the default pool.\n\nWhen accepting a connection fails with an OS error, such as when the process has no file descriptors left, the server logs the error and tries again after a wait that grows from 5 ms to 1 s.\n\nOn a plain TCP listener, a connection that starts with the HTTP/2 connection preface is served as cleartext HTTP/2; allow_h2c = false turns that off. A TLS listener offers HTTP/2 when its TLS.Config lists \"h2\" in alpn_protocols.\n\nThe constructor takes the keywords of serve! with timeouts in nanoseconds only; stream = true marks handler as a stream handler. Start the server with listen!(server).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Server"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Stream","page":"Server","text":"Stream\n\nOne HTTP exchange, read and written as an IO. A server passes one to each stream handler, and the client's open returns one.\n\nOn a server stream, startread returns the request, reading the stream reads the request body, and setstatus, setheader, and writes build the response. On a client stream, writes send the request body, and after startread reading the stream reads the response body.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Stream"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.listen!","page":"Server","text":"listen!(server) -> Server\n\nStart server, made with the Server constructor, in the background and return it.\n\n\n\n\n\nlisten!(handler, host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8080; kwargs...) -> Server\nlisten!(handler, port; kwargs...) -> Server\nlisten!(handler, listener; kwargs...) -> Server\n\nStart an HTTP server in the background and return its Server. handler(stream::Stream) reads each request from the Stream and writes the response to it. listen! takes the keywords of serve! except max_body_bytes, since a stream handler reads the body itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.listen!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.listen","page":"Server","text":"listen(handler, args...; kwargs...) -> Server\n\nLike listen!, but block until the server is closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.listen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.serve!","page":"Server","text":"serve!(handler, host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8080; kwargs...) -> Server\nserve!(handler, port; kwargs...) -> Server\nserve!(handler, listener; kwargs...) -> Server\n\nStart an HTTP server in the background and return its Server. handler(req::Request) returns the Response for each request; a handler that throws sends 500 Internal Server Error. Port 0 picks a free port. listener is a listener from Reseau.TCP.listen or Reseau.TLS.listen; pass a TLS listener to serve HTTPS. Server tasks run on Julia's :interactive thread pool; see Server.\n\nKeywords\n\nread_header_timeout, read_timeout, write_timeout, idle_timeout: timeouts in seconds, all off by default. Each also has a nanosecond form ending in _ns, such as read_timeout_ns. read_header_timeout limits reading a request's head, read_timeout then limits reading its body, and idle_timeout limits the wait for the next request on a kept-alive connection. The header and idle timeouts fall back to read_timeout when they are 0. A request whose head or body times out gets 408 Request Timeout.\nmax_body_bytes = 64 MiB: the largest request body serve! reads into memory; a larger one gets 413 Content Too Large. 0 means no limit.\nmax_header_bytes = 1 MiB: the largest request head.\nhttp2_settings: the HTTP2Settings for HTTP/2 connections.\nmax_concurrent_streams = 100: the most HTTP/2 streams a connection may have open; 0 means no limit.\nallow_h2c = true: serve cleartext HTTP/2 to clients that send the HTTP/2 connection preface.\nlistenany = false: true ignores port and listens on a free port.\nreuseaddr = true, backlog = 128: options for the listener that serve! creates; they do not apply to a listener argument.\n\nExtended help\n\nreadtimeout is a deprecated name for read_timeout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.serve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.serve","page":"Server","text":"serve(handler, args...; kwargs...) -> Server\n\nLike serve!, but block until the server is closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.serve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.streamhandler","page":"Server","text":"streamhandler(request_handler; max_body_bytes=64 MiB) -> stream handler\n\nReturn a stream handler, for listen!, that reads each request into memory, calls request_handler(req), and writes the response it returns. A request body larger than max_body_bytes gets 413 Content Too Large, as with serve!; 0 means no limit. A response body that is not in memory, such as a file from fileserver, is streamed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.streamhandler"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.servefile","page":"Server","text":"servefile(request, path; index_file=\"index.html\", redirect_canonical=true,\n          etag=nothing, cache_control=nothing) -> Response\n\nBuild the response to request for the file or directory at path, with the conditional and range handling of servecontent. A directory is served by its index_file. With redirect_canonical, a request for a directory without a trailing slash, a file with one, or the index file itself is redirected to the canonical path. Methods other than GET and HEAD get 405, a missing file 404, and an unsafe request path, such as one with .., 400.\n\netag may be :weak_stat, for an ETag made from the file's size and modification time, a function (path, stat) -> String, or a string; by default no ETag is sent. cache_control sets the Cache-Control header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.servefile"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.fileserver","page":"Server","text":"fileserver(root; index_file=\"index.html\", redirect_canonical=true, etag=nothing,\n           cache_control=nothing, spa_fallback=nothing) -> handler\n\nReturn a request handler that serves the files under the directory root with servefile, which describes the keywords. With spa_fallback, a missing path whose last segment does not look like a file name, such as /users/42, gets the file spa_fallback from root, as a single-page app needs; a missing path such as /app.js still gets 404. Throws ArgumentError when root is not an existing directory.\n\nSymbolic links under root are followed, also when they point outside it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.fileserver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.servecontent","page":"Server","text":"servecontent(request, source; name=\"\", size=nothing, modtime=nothing,\n             content_type=nothing, etag=nothing, headers=Headers(),\n             allow_ranges=true) -> Response\n\nBuild the response to request for source, an AbstractVector{UInt8} or a seekable IO; pass codeunits(str) for a string. Conditional requests (If-None-Match, If-Modified-Since, If-Match, If-Unmodified-Since) get 304 or 412 when they apply. A GET or HEAD request for one byte range gets 206 Partial Content, and an unsatisfiable range gets 416. Any other Range, such as several ranges or another unit, is ignored and the whole content is sent.\n\nname: a file name whose extension sets the Content-Type when content_type is not given. Without either, the type is detected from the content.\nsize: the length of source, needed when it cannot be found by seeking.\nmodtime: a DateTime sent as Last-Modified.\netag: the ETag value.\nheaders: more response headers.\nallow_ranges: answer Range requests.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.servecontent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.forceclose","page":"Server","text":"forceclose(server)\n\nStop server at once: stop accepting connections and close every open connection, including those with requests in progress.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.forceclose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.port","page":"Server","text":"port(server) -> Int\n\nReturn the port server listens on, or the configured port before it starts listening.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.port"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.startread","page":"Server","text":"startread(stream)\n\nStart reading from stream and return the message head.\n\nOn a client stream from open, this finishes sending the request and returns the Response with its status and headers. On a server stream, it returns the Request. Either way, read the body from stream itself, with read(stream) or readbytes!(stream, buf).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.startread"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.startwrite","page":"Server","text":"startwrite(stream) -> Response\n\nStart the response of a server-side Stream and return the response metadata. write(stream, data) starts it too.\n\nChunked and HTTP/2 responses send their head here. A fixed-length (Content-Length) HTTP/1 response holds its head and body so that a small response goes out in one write. Without an explicit startwrite, the whole body is held until closewrite, so the server can still send an error response if the handler throws or writes the wrong number of bytes. After startwrite, a body that grows past 4 KiB is sent as it is written, and a later failure closes the connection instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.startwrite"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.setstatus","page":"Server","text":"setstatus(stream, status) -> nothing\n\nSet the status of the response on a server stream. Call it before the first write; after that it throws ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.setstatus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.addtrailer","page":"Server","text":"addtrailer(stream, key => value) -> nothing\naddtrailer(stream, headers) -> nothing\n\nAdd trailers to the response on a server stream. They are sent after the body, so add them before closewrite(stream). Only chunked HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 responses carry trailers; an HTTP/1 response with a Content-Length drops them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.addtrailer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.closeread","page":"Server","text":"closeread(stream) -> Response\n\nStop reading from stream and return its Response: the response received on a client stream, or the response being written on a server stream. Body bytes that were not read are discarded, and the connection is then closed instead of reused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.closeread"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.peeraddr","page":"Server","text":"peeraddr(stream::Stream) -> Union{Nothing, Reseau.TCP.SocketAddr}\n\nReturn the client's address for a server stream, or nothing when it is not known. string(addr) gives \"ip:port\"; addr.ip is a tuple of the address bytes and addr.port is the port. Works over HTTP/1 and HTTP/2, with or without TLS. Throws ArgumentError for a client stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.peeraddr"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.Handler","page":"Server","text":"Handler\n\nThe name of the handler interface. Nothing subtypes or dispatches on it.\n\nA handler is a function f(req::HTTP.Request) -> HTTP.Response, run with serve!. A stream handler is a function f(stream::HTTP.Stream), run with listen!. Middleware used with stream handlers must take and return stream handlers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.Handler"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.Middleware","page":"Server","text":"Middleware\n\nThe name of the middleware interface. Nothing subtypes or dispatches on it. Middleware is a function that takes a Handler and returns a new one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.Middleware"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.Router","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.Router([_404], [_405], [middleware])\n\nA handler that calls the handler register!ed for the request's method and path. _404 handles a path with no route, and _405 a path whose routes do not accept the method; by default they return empty 404 and 405 responses. middleware, if given, wraps each handler as it is registered. A router works with request handlers (serve!) and with stream handlers (listen!).\n\nA route path is made of segments:\n\nexact segments, such as /users\nvariables, such as /users/{id}\nvariables with a regular expression, such as /files/{name:\\w+}\n*, which matches any one segment\n** at the end, which matches the rest of the path\n\nA handler reads the matched route with getroute and its variables with getparams and getparam.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.Router"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.Node","page":"Server","text":"Node\n\nInternal trie node used by Router to store exact, wildcard, and parameterized path segments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.Node"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.register!","page":"Server","text":"register!(router, method, path, handler) -> Nothing\nregister!(router, path, handler) -> Nothing\nregister!(handler, router, method, path) -> Nothing\nregister!(handler, router, path) -> Nothing\n\nAdd a route to router: requests with method and a path matching path go to handler. Without method, the route accepts every method. See Router for the path syntax.\n\nThe handler-first forms allow do blocks:\n\nHTTP.register!(router, \"GET\", \"/users/{id}\") do req\n    return HTTP.Response(200; body = HTTP.getparam(req, \"id\"))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.register!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.getroute","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.getroute(req) -> Union{Nothing, String}\n\nReturn the registered path, such as \"/users/{id}\", of the route that matched req, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.getroute"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.getparams","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.getparams(req) -> Union{Nothing, Dict{String, String}}\n\nReturn the path variables of the route that matched req, or nothing when no route matched or it has no variables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.getparams"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.getparam","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.getparam(req, name, default=nothing)\n\nReturn the path variable name of the route that matched req, or default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.getparam"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.getcookies","page":"Server","text":"HTTP.getcookies(req) -> Vector{Cookie}\n\nReturn the cookies in the Cookie header of req.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.getcookies"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.handlertimeout","page":"Server","text":"handlertimeout(timeout_s; status=503, body=\"handler timed out\", content_type=\"text/plain; charset=utf-8\")\n    -> middleware\n\nReturn middleware that answers with status, body, and content_type when a request handler takes longer than timeout_s seconds. The handler runs with a child RequestContext whose deadline is the earlier of the timeout and the request's own deadline. At the timeout that context is canceled, but the handler keeps running until it returns or checks the context. Only request handlers are supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.handlertimeout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.Handlers.logging_middleware","page":"Server","text":"logging_middleware(handler; logger=nothing, level=Logging.Info) -> handler\n\nWrap handler so that each request it handles logs one record, an access log.\n\nThe message reads like GET /users/1 200 0.412ms. The record has the request method and target, the response status, the handler's time in elapsed_ms, and the response body length in bytes when it is known without reading the body (nothing otherwise). Records are in the :access log group and logged at level, to logger when given and to the current logger otherwise. When the handler throws, the record is logged at Logging.Error with the exception and the status the server sends, and the exception is rethrown.\n\nIt wraps request handlers and stream handlers. Stream handler records also have the client's peer address from peeraddr. For a stream handler, the middleware finishes the response (closewrite) before logging, so a response that fails then, such as a body shorter than its Content-Length, is logged as that failure.\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(HTTP.Handlers.logging_middleware(router), \"127.0.0.1\", 8080)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Handlers.logging_middleware"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.SSEEvent","page":"Server","text":"SSEEvent(data; event=nothing, id=nothing, retry=nothing)\n\nOne server-sent event: what a server writes to an SSEStream, and what the client passes to an sse_callback.\n\nFields:\n\ndata: the event data; several data: lines are joined with \"\\n\"\nevent: the event type, or nothing\nid: the last event ID received, or nothing\nretry: the last reconnection time received, in milliseconds, or nothing\nfields: the event's fields by name\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.SSEEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/server/#HTTP.SSEStream","page":"Server","text":"SSEStream(; max_len=16*1024*1024)\n\nThe body of a server-sent event response from sse_stream. write(stream, event::SSEEvent) sends an event and close(stream) ends the response. max_len limits the size of one event, in bytes. Events the client has not read yet may hold up to four times max_len bytes, and at least 16 MiB: a write past that closes the stream and throws IOError, as it does once the client has gone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.SSEStream"},{"category":"function","location":"api/server/#HTTP.sse_stream","page":"Server","text":"sse_stream(status=200; max_len=16*1024*1024, kwargs...) -> Response\nsse_stream(f, status; max_len=16*1024*1024, kwargs...) -> Response\nsse_stream(response[, f]) -> SSEStream\n\nBuild a server-sent event response for a serve! handler to return. It has an SSEStream body and the headers Content-Type: text/event-stream and Cache-Control: no-cache. The other keywords are those of Response.\n\nWith a function f, usually a do block as in sse_stream(200) do stream, f(stream) runs on its own task and writes SSEEvents to the stream; the response ends when f returns. Without f, write events to response.body and close it to end the response.\n\nThe response forms take a Response whose body is already an SSEStream, set its headers, start f if given, and return the stream.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.sse_stream"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Client-Guide","page":"Client","text":"HTTP.request and the verb helpers such as HTTP.get and HTTP.post send a request and return an HTTP.Response with the whole body in memory. HTTP.open streams the response instead. An HTTP.Client holds connections and settings that you want to share across requests.","title":"Client Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Making-Requests","page":"Client","text":"using HTTP\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 0) do req\n    return HTTP.Response(200; body = \"$(req.method) $(req.target)\")\nend\nurl = \"http://127.0.0.1:$(HTTP.port(server))\"\n\nresp = HTTP.request(\"GET\", url * \"/hello\")\nresp.status        # 200\nString(resp.body)  # \"GET /hello\"\n\nHTTP.forceclose(server)\n\nHTTP.get, HTTP.head, HTTP.post, HTTP.put, HTTP.patch, HTTP.delete, HTTP.options, and HTTP.query call HTTP.request with a fixed method and accept the same keywords.\n\nA response with status 300 or above throws an HTTP.StatusError. Redirect responses (301, 302, 303, 307, and 308) are the exception. Pass status_exception = false to get the response instead.\n\nThe client follows up to 10 redirects. Set redirect = false or redirect_limit to change that. A Location header is resolved against the current URL as RFC 3986 specifies: . and .. segments are removed, while repeated slashes and percent-encoded bytes are kept. The path of the URL you pass is sent as written, and fragments are never sent.\n\nA redirect to another origin (a different scheme, host, or port) drops the Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization headers and the URL's credentials. Other headers are sent again, including an API key in a custom header such as X-Api-Key, as in curl and Go. Pass forwardheaders = false, or give the client a check_redirect function, to avoid that.","title":"Making Requests"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Request-Bodies","page":"Client","text":"Pass the body as the third positional argument or with the body keyword. Strings and byte vectors are sent as they are. Files and IOBuffers are read into memory first. Other IO values, and iterables of strings or byte chunks, are streamed with chunked transfer encoding.\n\nHTTP.jl has no JSON dependency. Serialize the payload yourself, for example with JSON.jl, and set the Content-Type header:\n\nusing HTTP, JSON\n\nresp = HTTP.post(\n    \"https://api.example.com/users\";\n    headers = [\"Content-Type\" => \"application/json\"],\n    body = JSON.json(Dict(\"name\" => \"alice\", \"age\" => 30)),\n)\nuser = JSON.parse(String(resp.body))\n\nA Dict or NamedTuple body is sent as an HTML form (application/x-www-form-urlencoded), and the Content-Type header is set for you:\n\nHTTP.post(\"https://example.com/login\", [], Dict(\"user\" => \"alice\", \"pw\" => \"s3cret\"))\n\nUse HTTP.Form for multipart/form-data, such as file uploads. An open file is sent with its file name:\n\nopen(\"upload.bin\") do file\n    HTTP.post(\"https://example.com/upload\", [], HTTP.Form(Dict(\"file\" => file, \"kind\" => \"binary\")))\nend","title":"Request Bodies"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#QUERY-requests","page":"Client","text":"RFC 10008 defines the QUERY method for safe, idempotent requests that carry a body. HTTP.query sends one:\n\nHTTP.query(\n    \"https://api.example.com/search\";\n    headers = [\"Content-Type\" => \"application/json\"],\n    body = \"\"\"{\"select\":[\"name\",\"email\"],\"limit\":10}\"\"\",\n)","title":"QUERY requests"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Query-Parameters","page":"Client","text":"The query keyword URL-encodes a Dict, NamedTuple, or vector of pairs and appends it to the URL's query string:\n\nHTTP.get(\"https://example.com/search\"; query = Dict(\"page\" => 2, \"limit\" => 10))\n# GET /search?limit=10&page=2\n\nA Dict has no order. Use a vector of pairs to fix the order or to repeat a key:\n\nHTTP.get(\"https://example.com/search\"; query = [\"tag\" => \"a\", \"tag\" => \"b\"])\n# GET /search?tag=a&tag=b\n\nquery adds to a query string that is already in the URL:\n\nHTTP.get(\"https://example.com/search?type=user\"; query = [\"page\" => 2])\n# GET /search?type=user&page=2","title":"Query Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Reading-the-Response-Body","page":"Client","text":"resp.body is a Vector{UInt8}. Convert it to text with String:\n\nresp = HTTP.get(\"https://example.com\")\ntext = String(resp.body)\n\nwarning: `String(resp.body)` empties `resp.body`\nString(::Vector{UInt8}) takes over the vector's memory instead of copying it, so resp.body is empty afterwards. Use String(copy(resp.body)) if you need the bytes again.","title":"Reading the Response Body"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Streaming-Responses","page":"Client","text":"HTTP.open sends the request and lets you read the response body as it arrives. It follows redirects and decompresses the body like HTTP.request, but without the max_decompressed_size limit: you decide how much to read.\n\nresp = HTTP.open(\"GET\", \"https://example.com\") do stream\n    while !eof(stream)\n        chunk = readavailable(stream)\n        # process chunk\n    end\nend\n\nThe do block form returns the final HTTP.Response, not the value of the block. Store anything you need from the block in a variable defined outside it. Call HTTP.startread(stream) inside the block to see the status and headers before you read the body.\n\nTo copy the body into an IO instead, pass it as response_stream. The response's body is then nothing:\n\nbuf = IOBuffer()\nresp = HTTP.get(\"https://example.com\"; response_stream = buf)\ntext = String(take!(buf))","title":"Streaming Responses"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Reusing-a-Client","page":"Client","text":"Calls without a client keyword share one default client, so they already reuse connections and share a cookie jar. Create your own HTTP.Client when a set of requests needs its own connection pool, cookies, or settings:\n\nclient = HTTP.Client(\n    transport = HTTP.Transport(max_idle_per_host = 4),\n    cookiejar = HTTP.CookieJar(),\n)\nresp = HTTP.get(client, \"https://example.com\")\nclose(client)\n\nEvery request function accepts the client as its first argument or as the client keyword. A closed client throws ArgumentError when you use it; isopen(client) tells you whether it is still open.\n\nSettings are split between the two types:\n\nHTTP.Transport owns connections: pool sizes (max_idle_per_host, max_idle_total, max_conns_per_host), idle_timeout_ns, TLS settings (tls_config), the proxy (proxy), the retry budget (retry_bucket), the source address (local_addr), and the response header limits (max_line_bytes and max_header_bytes).\nHTTP.Client owns request policy: the transport, the cookie jar, redirects (max_redirects, check_redirect), HTTP/2 (prefer_http2, http2_settings), and per-request defaults.\n\nA Client created without a transport reads its proxy from the HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, and NO_PROXY environment variables when it is created. A Transport you create yourself connects directly unless you pass proxy. Proxy URLs may use http://, socks5://, or socks5h://.","title":"Reusing a Client"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Per-client-defaults","page":"Client","text":"A client can carry defaults that apply to every request sent through it:\n\nclient = HTTP.Client(\n    default_headers = [\"User-Agent\" => \"MyApp/1.0\", \"X-API-Version\" => \"v2\"],\n    default_query = Dict(\"api_key\" => \"secret\"),\n    default_basicauth = \"alice\" => \"password\",\n    connect_timeout = 5,\n    request_timeout = 30,\n)\n\nHTTP.get(client, \"https://api.example.com/users\")\n\n# Per-call values replace the defaults they overlap.\nHTTP.get(client, \"https://api.example.com/users\"; headers = [\"X-API-Version\" => \"v1\"])\n\ndefault_headers are added unless the call sets the same header.\ndefault_query is merged with the call's query; keys in the call replace the default values for the same key.\ndefault_basicauth is used unless the call passes basicauth or an Authorization header.\nThe timeouts (connect_timeout, request_timeout, response_header_timeout, read_idle_timeout, write_idle_timeout) apply when the call passes no value or 0.","title":"Per-client defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Binding-to-a-local-address","page":"Client","text":"local_addr sets the source IP address of a client's connections, which picks the outgoing interface on a host with several. Pass an IP address string, or a Reseau.TCP.SocketAddrV4 or SocketAddrV6 to also fix the source port:\n\nclient = HTTP.Client(local_addr = \"192.0.2.10\")\n\nThe address must belong to a local interface. Interface names such as \"en0\" throw an ArgumentError. When you pass your own transport, set local_addr on the Transport instead.","title":"Binding to a local address"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Timeouts","page":"Client","text":"Each timeout keyword bounds one part of a request. All values are in seconds.\n\nconnect_timeout bounds DNS lookup, the TCP connect, the proxy CONNECT or SOCKS5 handshake, the TLS handshake, and HTTP/2 connection setup. It defaults to 30.\nrequest_timeout bounds the whole call.\nresponse_header_timeout bounds the wait for the response headers after the request is sent.\nread_idle_timeout bounds the time between reads, including the wait for the response headers.\nwrite_idle_timeout bounds the time between writes.\nexpect_continue_timeout sets how long an HTTP/1 upload with an Expect: 100-continue header waits for 100 Continue before it sends the body anyway.\n\nrequest_timeout, response_header_timeout, read_idle_timeout, and write_idle_timeout are off unless you set them. A timeout throws HTTP.TimeoutError.\n\nresp = HTTP.get(\n    \"https://example.com\";\n    connect_timeout = 2,\n    response_header_timeout = 5,\n    read_idle_timeout = 30,\n)\n\nHTTP.open takes the same keywords. HTTP.WebSockets.open takes all of them except expect_continue_timeout.","title":"Timeouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Retries","page":"Client","text":"By default a request is retried up to 4 times (retries = 4) when all of these hold:\n\nthe method is GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, TRACE, QUERY, PUT, or DELETE, or the request has an Idempotency-Key or X-Idempotency-Key header (pass retry_non_idempotent = true to retry other requests)\nthe body can be sent again, which is true unless it is streamed from an IO or an iterable\nthe attempt failed with a network error, such as a refused or reset connection, or the response status is 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, or 504\n\nRetries wait a random, exponentially growing delay, or the delay that a 429 or 503 response asks for in its Retry-After header, up to 20 seconds. Pass retry = false to turn retries off. Each Transport also has a HTTP.RetryBucket that limits how many retries a burst of failures can trigger.\n\nPass retry_if to make your own decision:\n\nfunction retry_if(attempt, err, req, resp)\n    err !== nothing && return attempt <= 2      # failed before a response\n    return resp.status in (429, 503) && attempt <= 3\nend\n\nHTTP.get(\"https://example.com\"; retry_if = retry_if)\n\nretry_if(attempt, err, req, resp) gets the one-based number of the attempt that just finished. For a failure before any response, err is an HTTP.RequestRetryError whose err.err field holds the underlying exception, and resp is nothing. For a response, err is nothing and resp is the response. Return true to retry, false to stop, or nothing to use the default rules. A body that cannot be sent again is never retried.","title":"Retries"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Cancellation-and-Deadlines","page":"Client","text":"Pass an HTTP.RequestContext as context to control a request from another task. HTTP.cancel!(ctx) aborts the request, which then throws HTTP.CanceledError:\n\nctx = HTTP.RequestContext()\ntask = Threads.@spawn HTTP.get(\"https://example.com/slow\"; context = ctx)\nHTTP.cancel!(ctx)\nfetch(task)  # throws a TaskFailedException wrapping an HTTP.CanceledError\n\nA context can also carry an absolute deadline, set with HTTP.RequestContext(deadline_ns = time_ns() + 5_000_000_000) or HTTP.set_deadline!. A request still running at the deadline throws HTTP.TimeoutError. HTTP.request, the verb helpers, HTTP.open, and HTTP.do! all accept context.","title":"Cancellation and Deadlines"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Debugging-Requests","page":"Client","text":"verbose = 1 prints a line for each attempt, response, retry, and redirect, and one for the result. verbose = 2 also prints the request and response heads:\n\nHTTP.get(\"http://127.0.0.1:8080/v\"; verbose = 2)\n\n[http] request attempt 1 GET http://127.0.0.1:8080/v via h1\n[http] request\nGET /v HTTP/1.1\nHost: 127.0.0.1:8080\nAccept-Encoding: gzip, deflate\nUser-Agent: HTTP.jl/<version>\n[http] response attempt 1 200 for http://127.0.0.1:8080/v\n[http] response\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Length: 6\n[http] done 200 for http://127.0.0.1:8080/v\n\nTo collect the same information in code, pass a trace callback. It receives these events: HTTP.RequestEvent, HTTP.ResponseHeadEvent, HTTP.RetryEvent, HTTP.RetrySkippedEvent, HTTP.RedirectEvent, and HTTP.DoneEvent.","title":"Debugging Requests"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/client/#Avoiding-Copies","page":"Client","text":"A byte vector or contiguous byte view passed as the body is sent without being copied, and retries send the same bytes again. Do not change the bytes until the call returns.\n\nPass copyheaders = false with an HTTP.Headers collection to let HTTP.request or HTTP.open use it without copying it first. Do not touch the collection until the call returns, and do not share it between requests that run at the same time; its contents afterwards are unspecified. Other header inputs are always copied.\n\npayload = read(\"upload.bin\")\nheaders = HTTP.Headers([\"Content-Type\" => \"application/octet-stream\"])\nresp = HTTP.put(\"https://example.com/upload\", headers, payload; copyheaders = false)\n\nA byte vector or writable byte view passed as response_stream receives the body directly. It must be large enough for the whole body, or the call throws ArgumentError. A Vector{UInt8} is resized to the body length. A response with status 300 or above, including a redirect that is not followed, leaves it untouched: up to 1 MiB of that body is returned in resp.body instead, so a StatusError still carries it.","title":"Avoiding Copies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/client/#Client-API","page":"Client","text":"Pages = [\"api/client.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"Client API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/client/#Client-Types-and-Operations","page":"Client","text":"","title":"Client Types and Operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/client/#Request-Trace-Events","page":"Client","text":"","title":"Request Trace Events"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.Transport","page":"Client","text":"Transport(; tls_config=nothing, proxy=nothing, retry_bucket=RetryBucket(),\n          max_idle_per_host=2, max_idle_total=64, max_conns_per_host=0,\n          idle_timeout_ns=90_000_000_000, max_header_bytes=1024*1024,\n          max_line_bytes=64*1024, local_addr=nothing)\n\nThe connection pool of a Client, with the settings for opening connections.\n\ntls_config: a Reseau.TLS.Config for https:// connections.\nproxy: a ProxyConfig, a proxy URL, or nothing to connect directly. A Client created without a transport uses ProxyFromEnvironment instead.\nretry_bucket: the RetryBucket that limits retries, or nothing.\nmax_idle_per_host, max_idle_total: how many idle HTTP/1 connections to keep for one host and in all.\nmax_conns_per_host: the most HTTP/1 connections to one host, counting idle, busy, and opening ones; further requests wait for one. 0 means no limit.\nidle_timeout_ns: how long, in nanoseconds, an idle HTTP/1 or HTTP/2 connection stays reusable; 0 means no limit. Keep it below the idle timeout of NATs and load balancers on the path, often a few minutes, so that requests are not sent on connections they dropped. A response header Keep-Alive: timeout=N shortens it to N - 1 seconds for that connection.\nmax_line_bytes, max_header_bytes: the longest HTTP/1 response line and the largest response head (and chunked trailers); a longer one fails the request with a ProtocolError. max_line_bytes defaults to the smaller of 64 KiB and max_header_bytes. Raise it for servers that send very long header lines, such as large Content-Security-Policy values.\nlocal_addr: the source IP address of connections, as a string or a Reseau.TCP.SocketAddrV4/SocketAddrV6.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Transport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.Client","page":"Client","text":"Client(; transport=Transport(proxy=ProxyFromEnvironment()), cookiejar=CookieJar(),\n       max_redirects=10, check_redirect=nothing, prefer_http2=true,\n       http2_settings=HTTP2Settings(), default_headers=[], default_query=nothing,\n       default_basicauth=nothing, connect_timeout=0, request_timeout=0,\n       response_header_timeout=0, read_idle_timeout=0, write_idle_timeout=0,\n       local_addr=nothing)\n\nAn HTTP client: a connection pool plus the settings and cookies that requests sent through it share. Pass it to request, the verb helpers, or open as the first argument or as the client keyword. Calls without a client use a shared default Client. close(client) closes its connections; using a closed client throws ArgumentError.\n\ntransport: the Transport that owns the connections. The default one takes its proxy from the environment.\ncookiejar: the CookieJar for this client, or nothing for no cookies.\nmax_redirects: the most redirects one request follows.\ncheck_redirect: a function (response, request, location) -> Bool; return false to stop at a redirect and return it.\nprefer_http2: try HTTP/2 for https:// URLs. When an origin turns out to speak only HTTP/1.1, the client remembers that until close_idle_connections!. protocol = :h2 on a call always tries HTTP/2.\nhttp2_settings: the HTTP2Settings for HTTP/2 connections.\ndefault_headers: headers added to each request that does not set them.\ndefault_query: query parameters added to each URL; the call's query replaces those with the same key.\ndefault_basicauth: user => password used unless the call passes basicauth or an Authorization header.\nconnect_timeout, request_timeout, response_header_timeout, read_idle_timeout, write_idle_timeout: timeouts in seconds, used when the call passes no value or 0. 0 here means no default.\nlocal_addr: the source IP address for connections, as a string or a Reseau.TCP.SocketAddrV4/SocketAddrV6. With your own transport, set it on the Transport instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.Client"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.RetryBucket","page":"Client","text":"RetryBucket(; backoff_scale_factor_ms=25, max_backoff_secs=20, capacity=500)\n\nA retry budget shared by the requests of a Transport, so that a burst of failures does not become a burst of retries. Each host has its own budget of capacity. A retry reserves part of it; the reservation comes back when the retried attempt gets a response that is not worth retrying, and is kept, in whole or in part, when the attempt fails again. Each request that succeeds without a retry earns back one unit. When a host's budget runs out, failures are not retried, and a RetrySkippedEvent with reason :retry_bucket reports it.\n\nRetries wait a random time of up to backoff_scale_factor_ms * 2^(attempt - 1) milliseconds, and at most max_backoff_secs seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RetryBucket"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.RequestRetryError","page":"Client","text":"RequestRetryError(err)\n\nWhat a retry_if function receives as err when an attempt failed without a response. err.err is the exception.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RequestRetryError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.retry_attempts","page":"Client","text":"retry_attempts(request) -> Int\nretry_attempts(response) -> Int\n\nReturn how many times the client retried the request, 0 when the first attempt was the only one. The count is also stored as request.context[:retryattempt].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.retry_attempts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.isrecoverable","page":"Client","text":"isrecoverable(err::Exception) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when err is a transient failure that the client's default retry rules retry: a connection that was reset or closed early, a socket error, a malformed response, a connect or TLS handshake timeout, or an HTTP/2 stream the server refused. An expired deadline and other errors return false. err may also be the RequestRetryError that a retry_if function receives.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.isrecoverable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.roundtrip!","page":"Client","text":"roundtrip!(transport, address, request; secure=false, server_name=nothing) -> Response\n\nSend request to address (\"host:port\") over one HTTP/1 connection from transport, without redirects, cookies, or the client's retry rules. secure = true uses TLS, with server_name for SNI and certificate checks. The response body is a stream; read it with body_read! and close it with body_close!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.roundtrip!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.request","page":"Client","text":"request(method, url, headers=[], body=nothing; kwargs...) -> Response\nrequest(trace, method, url, headers=[], body=nothing; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend an HTTP request and return the Response, with the whole body in response.body as a Vector{UInt8}.\n\nurl is a string or URI. headers is anything Headers accepts. body may be a String, a byte vector, an IO, a Dict or NamedTuple (sent as a URL-encoded form), a Form, an iterable of string or byte chunks, or an AbstractBody. headers and body can also be passed as keywords. Unless the request sets them, HTTP.jl sends User-Agent: HTTP.jl/<version> and Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate.\n\nA response with status 300 or above throws a StatusError, except for redirect responses (301, 302, 303, 307, and 308). A timeout throws a TimeoutError, and a failure to connect throws an error such as ConnectError or DNSError.\n\nwarning: `String(response.body)` empties `response.body`\nString(::Vector{UInt8}) takes over the vector's memory instead of copying it, so response.body is empty afterwards. Use String(copy(response.body)) if you need the bytes again.\n\nKeywords\n\nRequest and response:\n\nquery: a Dict, NamedTuple, vector of pairs, or string to add to the URL's query string.\nbasicauth: credentials as user => password or (user, password). An Authorization header takes precedence; credentials in the URL are used when neither is given. In a URL, percent-encode characters such as /, ?, #, space, and `, as inhttps://user:p%2Fss@host/`.\nstatus_exception = true: throw StatusError for a failing response.\nresponse_stream: an IO or byte vector that receives the body instead of response.body, which is then nothing for an IO and the filled vector or view otherwise. A byte vector must be large enough for the body, and is resized to it if it is a Vector{UInt8}. A response with status 300 or above, including a redirect that is not followed, leaves a byte vector untouched and puts up to 1 MiB of its body (or max_decompressed_size, if smaller) in response.body.\ndecompress = true: decompress gzip and deflate responses. false returns the body as sent and does not send Accept-Encoding.\nmax_decompressed_size = 64 MiB: the largest decompressed body; a larger one throws DecompressionLimitError. 0 means no limit.\nsse_callback: for a server-sent event stream, call f(event) or f(stream, event) with each SSEEvent instead of reading the body. max_sse_line_bytes = 1 MiB and max_sse_event_bytes = 16 MiB limit the size of a line and an event; 0 means no limit.\ncopyheaders = true: false lets the request use an HTTP.Headers collection without copying it. Do not touch it until the call returns; its contents afterwards are unspecified.\ncanonicalize_headers = false: true sends header names in canonical_header_key form. Otherwise names are sent as given, and HTTP/2 sends them in lowercase.\n\nRedirects:\n\nredirect = true: follow redirects.\nredirect_limit: the most redirects to follow; defaults to the client's max_redirects, 10 by default. With 0, the redirect response is returned.\nredirect_method: the method for following a 301 or 302 redirect, or :same to keep the original method. By default GET is used, except that HEAD and QUERY are kept. A 303 always uses GET, and 307 and 308 keep the method.\nforwardheaders = true: send the request's headers again when following a redirect. Authorization, Cookie, and Proxy-Authorization are dropped when the redirect goes to another origin; other headers, such as X-Api-Key, are sent there too.\n\nRetries (see the client guide for the default rules):\n\nretry = true: retry failed attempts. An idempotent request that fails on a reused connection the server already closed is resent either way.\nretries = 4: the most retries after the first attempt.\nretry_non_idempotent = false: also retry methods other than GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, TRACE, QUERY, PUT, and DELETE.\nretry_if: a function (attempt, err, req, resp) that returns true to retry, false to stop, or nothing for the default rules. For a failure without a response, err is a RequestRetryError and resp is nothing; for a response, err is nothing. A body that cannot be sent again is never retried.\nrespect_retry_after = true: wait as long as a 429 or 503 response's Retry-After header asks.\nretry_bucket = true: use the transport's RetryBucket to limit retries; pass false to skip it, or another RetryBucket.\n\nTimeouts, in seconds:\n\nconnect_timeout: DNS lookup, TCP connect, proxy handshake, TLS handshake, and HTTP/2 setup. Defaults to the client's connect_timeout, or 30.\nrequest_timeout: the whole call.\nresponse_header_timeout: the wait for the response headers.\nread_idle_timeout: the time between reads, including the wait for the response headers.\nwrite_idle_timeout: the time between writes.\nexpect_continue_timeout: how long a request with an Expect: 100-continue header waits for 100 Continue before it sends the body anyway, 1 second by default. 0 sends the body without waiting.\ncontext: a RequestContext for cancellation and an absolute deadline.\n\nConnections:\n\nclient: the Client to use; defaults to a shared client.\nproxy: a proxy URL, a ProxyConfig, or nothing to connect directly. Cannot be combined with client; set the proxy on its Transport.\ncookies = true: send and store cookies with the cookie jar. false turns cookies off, and a Dict adds name/value pairs. Cookies from the jar are merged into a Cookie header set on the request.\ncookiejar: the CookieJar to use; defaults to the client's jar, or HTTP.COOKIEJAR for calls without a client.\nrequire_ssl_verification = true: false skips certificate checks, for testing only. Cannot be combined with client.\nprotocol = :auto: :h1 or :h2 to force HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2.\n\nDebugging:\n\nverbose: 1 (or true) prints a line for each attempt, response, retry, redirect, and the result; 2 also prints the request and response heads.\ntrace: a function called with each RequestEvent, ResponseHeadEvent, RetryEvent, RetrySkippedEvent, RedirectEvent, and DoneEvent.\n\nExtended help\n\nThese HTTP.jl 1.x keywords are accepted but have no effect, and log a warning: pool, retry_delays, retry_check, sslconfig, socket_type_tls, detect_content_type, observelayers, logerrors, and logtag. The deprecated readtimeout sets read_idle_timeout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.request"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#Base.get","page":"Client","text":"get(headers, key, default)\n\nDict-style get on Headers. Returns the first value for key, including an empty value, or default if the header is absent.\n\n\n\n\n\nget(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.get"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.head","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.head"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.query","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.query"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.post","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.post"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.put","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.put"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.patch","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.patch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.delete","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.delete"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.options","page":"Client","text":"get(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\nhead(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\noptions(url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Response\npost(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nput(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\npatch(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\ndelete(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\nquery(url, [headers], [body]; kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request with the method in the function's name: HTTP.get(url) is HTTP.request(\"GET\", url). Each takes the keywords of request, including headers and body, and also accepts a Client as its first argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.open","page":"Client","text":"open(method, url, headers=[]; kwargs...) -> Stream\nopen(f, method, url, headers=[]; status_exception=true, kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a request and stream its body and response through a Stream. Write the request body to the stream, call startread to finish the request and get the response status and headers, then read the response body from the stream like any IO.\n\nWith a function f, open calls f(stream), finishes the exchange, and returns the final Response, not the value of f. It throws a StatusError for a failing response unless status_exception = false. Without f, close the stream with closewrite and closeread when you are done.\n\nopen follows redirects, retries, decompresses, and applies timeouts like request, and takes its keywords except body, response_stream, sse_callback, trace, verbose, and the size limits.\n\nbuf = IOBuffer()\nresponse = HTTP.open(\"GET\", \"https://example.com\") do stream\n    write(buf, stream)  # copy the body as it arrives\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.open"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.do!","page":"Client","text":"do!(client, address, request; secure=false, server_name=nothing, protocol=:auto,\n    redirect_limit=nothing, redirect_method=nothing, forwardheaders=true,\n    cookies=true, cookiejar=nothing, context=nothing) -> Response\n\nSend a prepared request to address (\"host:port\") through client, following redirects and using cookies like request, but without retries or status errors. secure = true connects with TLS.\n\nThe response body is a stream: read it with body_read! and close it with body_close!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.do!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#Base.get!","page":"Client","text":"get!(client, address, target; secure=false, protocol=:auto, kwargs...) -> Response\n\nSend a GET request for target to address with do!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.get!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/client/#HTTP.@client","page":"Client","text":"HTTP.@client request_middleware\nHTTP.@client request_middleware stream_middleware\n\nDefine request, the verb helpers (get, post, ...), and open in the calling module so that they pass through middleware before reaching HTTP.jl.\n\nA middleware is a function mw(next) -> f. Request middleware returns f(method, url, headers, body; kwargs...), which handles request and the verb helpers. Stream middleware returns f(method, url, headers; kwargs...), which handles open. Each position takes one middleware or a tuple of them; in (outer, inner), outer runs first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.@client"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.close_idle_connections!","page":"Client","text":"close_idle_connections!()\nclose_idle_connections!(client::HTTP.Client)\nclose_idle_connections!(transport::HTTP.Transport)\n\nClose the idle connections in a pool; requests in progress are not affected. Without an argument, it uses the shared client of calls that pass no client.\n\nThe Client and no-argument forms also close idle HTTP/2 connections, and forget which origins answered with HTTP/1.1, so later requests try HTTP/2 with them again. The Transport form covers only its HTTP/1 connections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.close_idle_connections!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.idle_connection_count","page":"Client","text":"idle_connection_count(transport; key=nothing) -> Int\n\nReturn the number of idle HTTP/1 connections in transport, or only those for key, a scheme and address such as \"https://example.com:443\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.idle_connection_count"},{"category":"function","location":"api/client/#HTTP.isaborted","page":"Client","text":"isaborted(stream) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when the response on a client stream ended in an error, so its connection cannot be reused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.isaborted"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.RequestEvent","page":"Client","text":"RequestEvent\n\nTrace event emitted immediately before a high-level request attempt is sent.\n\nFields:\n\nrequest: request head/body metadata for the attempt\nurl: absolute request URL for the attempt\nattempt: 1-based request attempt number\nredirect_count: number of redirects already followed before this attempt\nprotocol: :h1 or :h2 for the selected wire protocol\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RequestEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.ResponseHeadEvent","page":"Client","text":"ResponseHeadEvent\n\nTrace event emitted after response headers are available for a successful request attempt and before the body is fully consumed.\n\nFields:\n\nresponse: response head metadata for the attempt\nurl: absolute request URL for the attempt\nattempt: 1-based request attempt number\nredirect_count: number of redirects followed before this response\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.ResponseHeadEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.RetryEvent","page":"Client","text":"RetryEvent\n\nTrace event emitted when the high-level request path schedules another attempt.\n\nFields:\n\nrequest: request metadata that will be retried\nurl: absolute request URL for the attempt being retried\nattempt: current 1-based attempt number\nnext_attempt: next 1-based attempt number\nredirect_count: redirects already followed when the retry decision was made\ndelay_ns: delay before the next attempt in nanoseconds\nresponse: retry-triggering response, or nothing for request-path failures\nerr: retry-triggering exception, or nothing for response-based retries\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RetryEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.RetrySkippedEvent","page":"Client","text":"RetrySkippedEvent\n\nTrace event emitted when the retry policy wanted to retry an attempt but the retry was not armed, so the attempt's failure becomes the request's outcome. Without this event a denied retry is indistinguishable from a non-retryable failure.\n\nFields:\n\nrequest: request metadata for the attempt that will not be retried\nurl: absolute request URL for the attempt\nattempt: current 1-based attempt number\nredirect_count: redirects already followed when the retry decision was made\nreason: :retry_bucket when the transport's RetryBucket denied retry capacity; :deadline when the request deadline preempted the retry backoff\nresponse: retry-triggering response, or nothing for request-path failures\nerr: retry-triggering exception, or nothing for response-based retries\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RetrySkippedEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.RedirectEvent","page":"Client","text":"RedirectEvent\n\nTrace event emitted when a redirect response is accepted and a follow-up request is about to be issued.\n\nFields:\n\nrequest: original request metadata that produced the redirect response\nresponse: redirect response head\nfrom_url: original absolute request URL\nto_url: resolved redirect target URL\nredirect_count: 1-based redirect count after accepting this redirect\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.RedirectEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/client/#HTTP.DoneEvent","page":"Client","text":"DoneEvent\n\nTrace event emitted when a high-level request call finishes, either with a final response or an exception.\n\nFields:\n\nresponse: final response, or nothing if the request failed before one was produced\nerr: terminal exception, or nothing when the request completed successfully\nurl: absolute request URL for the overall call\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.DoneEvent"},{"category":"section","location":"api/websockets/#WebSockets-API","page":"WebSockets","text":"Pages = [\"api/websockets.md\"]\nDepth = 2","title":"WebSockets API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/websockets/#Module-and-Core-Types","page":"WebSockets","text":"","title":"Module and Core Types"},{"category":"section","location":"api/websockets/#Client-Operations","page":"WebSockets","text":"","title":"Client Operations"},{"category":"section","location":"api/websockets/#Server-Operations","page":"WebSockets","text":"","title":"Server Operations"},{"category":"module","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets","page":"WebSockets","text":"HTTP.WebSockets\n\nWebSocket clients and servers. open connects to a server, and listen! starts one; upgrade serves WebSockets from an HTTP.listen! handler. Each connection is a WebSocket.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets"},{"category":"type","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.Conn","page":"WebSockets","text":"Conn\n\nLow-level WebSocket codec connection state.\n\nThis is primarily useful for advanced integrations that need direct access to frame-level state. Most client and server code should work with WebSocket instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.Conn"},{"category":"type","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.CloseFrameBody","page":"WebSockets","text":"CloseFrameBody(code, reason=\"\")\n\nStructured close payload carrying the WebSocket close status code and optional UTF-8 reason text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.CloseFrameBody"},{"category":"type","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.WebSocketError","page":"WebSockets","text":"WebSocketError\n\nRaised when a WebSocket is closed or breaks the protocol. err.message is the CloseFrameBody: codes 1000, 1001, and 1005 mean a normal close.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.WebSocketError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.WebSocket","page":"WebSockets","text":"WebSocket\n\nOne WebSocket connection, returned by open and passed to the handler of a WebSocket server. Use send, receive, ping, pong, and close(ws). Iterating over it yields each message until the peer closes the connection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.WebSocket"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.open","page":"WebSockets","text":"open(url; kwargs...) -> WebSocket\nopen(f, url; kwargs...)\n\nConnect to the WebSocket server at url, a ws:// or wss:// URL, and return the WebSocket. With a function f, open calls f(ws), closes the connection with status 1000 when f returns, and returns the value of f.\n\nheaders, query, subprotocols: the handshake's headers, query parameters, and offered subprotocols.\nmaxframesize = 16 MiB, maxfragmentation = 1024: the largest incoming frame, and the most frames in one message. Messages that have arrived but not been received may hold up to four times maxframesize bytes; past that the connection is closed with status 1008.\ncompress = false: offer permessage-deflate compression (RFC 7692), used when the server accepts it.\nconnect_timeout = 30, request_timeout, response_header_timeout, read_idle_timeout, write_idle_timeout: handshake timeouts in seconds, as for HTTP.request. read_idle_timeout keeps applying after the handshake: if nothing arrives for that long, the connection closes.\nclient, proxy, redirect, redirect_limit, redirect_method, forwardheaders, cookies, cookiejar, require_ssl_verification: as for HTTP.request.\n\n\n\n\n\nopen(io::IO; target=\"/\", host=\"\", headers=[], subprotocols=String[],\n     maxframesize=16 MiB, maxfragmentation=1024) -> WebSocket\nopen(f, io::IO; kwargs...)\n\nDo the client handshake over io, a stream that is already connected, such as a TCPSocket or a TLS stream, instead of connecting to a URL. target is the request path, and host the Host header, which is left out when empty. open never closes io; with f, it sends a close frame over io when f returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.open"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.send","page":"WebSockets","text":"send(ws, message) -> Int\n\nSend one text or binary message on ws.\n\nmessage may be an AbstractString, an AbstractVector{UInt8}, or an iterable of chunks. Iterable inputs are sent as one fragmented message and the returned value is the total payload bytes sent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.send"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.receive","page":"WebSockets","text":"receive(ws) -> Union{String, Vector{UInt8}}\n\nReceive the next complete message from ws.\n\nText messages are returned as String; binary messages are returned as Vector{UInt8}. Throws WebSocketError once the connection has closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.receive"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.ping","page":"WebSockets","text":"ping(ws, data=UInt8[]) -> nothing\n\nSend a WebSocket ping control frame with optional payload bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.ping"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.pong","page":"WebSockets","text":"pong(ws, data=UInt8[]) -> nothing\n\nSend a WebSocket pong control frame with optional payload bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.pong"},{"category":"type","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.Server","page":"WebSockets","text":"Server(; network=\"tcp\", address=\"127.0.0.1:0\", handler, tls_config=nothing, ...)\n\nWebSocket server handle returned by listen! and serve!.\n\nHold onto the returned value so you can inspect its bound address with server_addr, wait on it with wait(server), or stop it with forceclose.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.Server"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.listen!","page":"WebSockets","text":"listen!(handler, host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8080; kwargs...) -> Server\n\nStart a WebSocket server in the background and return its Server. handler(ws::WebSocket) runs for each connection. Port 0, or listenany = true, picks a free port; server_addr returns the address.\n\ntls_config: a Reseau.TLS.Config to serve wss://.\nsubprotocols: the subprotocols the server accepts.\ncheck_origin: a function (request) or (request, origin) that returns true to accept a handshake. By default a handshake with an Origin header is accepted only when the origin's scheme, host, and port match the server.\ncompress = false: accept permessage-deflate compression (RFC 7692) from clients that offer it.\nmaxframesize = 16 MiB, maxfragmentation = 1024: the largest incoming frame, and the most frames in one message. Messages that have arrived but not been received may hold up to four times maxframesize bytes; past that the connection is closed with status 1008.\nread_header_timeout, read_timeout, idle_timeout: timeouts in seconds, all off by default, as for HTTP.serve!; each also has a nanosecond form ending in _ns. read_header_timeout limits the handshake, from accepting the connection to the end of the upgrade request, and idle_timeout closes a connection on which nothing arrives for that long. Both fall back to read_timeout when they are 0.\n\nWebSocket server tasks use Julia's :interactive thread pool. Start Julia with at least one interactive thread, such as --threads=4,1, to isolate server work from non-yielding tasks on the default pool. Without an interactive thread, Julia falls back to the default pool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.listen!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.listen","page":"WebSockets","text":"listen(handler, host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8080; kwargs...) -> Server\n\nStart a WebSocket server and block until it exits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.listen"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.serve!","page":"WebSockets","text":"serve! is another name for listen!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.serve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.upgrade","page":"WebSockets","text":"upgrade(f, stream::HTTP.Stream; subprotocols=String[], check_origin=nothing,\n        maxframesize=16 MiB, maxfragmentation=1024, compress=false)\n\nAnswer a WebSocket handshake on an HTTP/1.1 server stream and call f(ws::WebSocket). Use it in an HTTP.listen! handler to serve WebSockets and ordinary HTTP routes from one server. Check the request with isupgrade first, and call upgrade before writing to the stream:\n\nHTTP.listen!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do stream\n    if HTTP.WebSockets.isupgrade(stream.message)\n        HTTP.WebSockets.upgrade(stream) do ws\n            for msg in ws\n                HTTP.WebSockets.send(ws, msg)\n            end\n        end\n    else\n        HTTP.setstatus(stream, 200)\n        HTTP.startwrite(stream)\n        write(stream, \"ok\")\n    end\nend\n\nupgrade returns when f does, and the connection is then closed. The keywords work as for listen!. A stream on an HTTP/2 connection throws WebSocketError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.upgrade"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.isupgrade","page":"WebSockets","text":"isupgrade(message) -> Bool\n\nReturn true when message is a WebSocket upgrade. For a request this checks for a valid client upgrade handshake — use it to guard upgrade inside an HTTP.listen! stream handler. For a response it checks for a 101 Switching Protocols handshake response.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.isupgrade"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.server_addr","page":"WebSockets","text":"server_addr(server) -> String\n\nReturn the host:port address currently bound by server.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.server_addr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/websockets/#HTTP.WebSockets.forceclose","page":"WebSockets","text":"forceclose(server) -> nothing\n\nImmediately stop accepting new WebSocket connections and close active sessions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HTTP.WebSockets.forceclose"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Migration-From-HTTP.jl-1.x","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"HTTP.jl 2.0 is a breaking release with a new HTTP stack underneath. Code that calls HTTP.get, HTTP.post, HTTP.request, HTTP.serve!, HTTP.Router, and HTTP.WebSockets usually needs only small changes. Code that used parser, connection-pool, layer-stack, or other internal APIs has to move to the public API. HTTP.jl 2.0 requires Julia 1.10 or later.\n\nThe snippets below assume using HTTP and an existing url.","title":"Migration From HTTP.jl 1.x"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Checklist","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"Read data written with response_stream = io from io; resp.body is nothing.\nReplace HTTP.download with Downloads.download or a response_stream file.\nRead text from a server request with String(req.body).\nUse HTTP.get_request_context(req) for cancellation and deadlines.\nReplace pool with a long-lived HTTP.Client.\nReplace retry_delays and retry_check with retries, retry_if, and HTTP.RetryBucket.\nReplace readtimeout with the timeout keyword you need.\nReplace sslconfig with a Transport's tls_config.\nReplace HTTP.Exceptions.StatusError and friends with the top-level names, and HTTP.RequestError with the underlying exception.\nReplace Sockets.getpeername(stream) with HTTP.peeraddr(stream).\nStart servers with an interactive thread, such as julia --threads=4,1.\nRe-test redirects, retries, proxies, cookies, streaming, WebSockets, server-sent events, and HTTP/2.","title":"Checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Response-Bodies","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"HTTP.request and the verb helpers still return the whole body as a Vector{UInt8} in resp.body. With response_stream = io, 1.x also stored io in resp.body. In 2.0 resp.body is nothing; read the data from the io you passed:\n\nopen(\"payload.bin\", \"w\") do io\n    resp = HTTP.get(url; response_stream = io)\n    @assert resp.body === nothing\nend","title":"Response Bodies"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#HTTP.download","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"HTTP.jl 2.0 has no download function. HTTP.download(url, path) still runs, because every module can see Base.download, but it calls Base.download, which uses the Downloads standard library. It accepts none of HTTP.jl's keywords; passing one throws a MethodError.\n\nUse Downloads.download to copy a URL to a file:\n\nusing Downloads\n\nDownloads.download(url, \"payload.bin\")\n\nStream the response into a file when you need HTTP.jl options such as headers, retries, proxies, or a Client:\n\nopen(\"payload.bin\", \"w\") do io\n    HTTP.get(url; response_stream = io)\nend","title":"HTTP.download"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Requests,-Responses,-and-Headers","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The 1.x positional constructors still work:\n\nreq = HTTP.Request(\"POST\", \"/widgets\", [\"Content-Type\" => \"text/plain\"], \"hello\")\nresp = HTTP.Response(201, [\"Location\" => \"/widgets/1\"], \"created\")\n\nThe keyword forms take the same values plus trailers, protocol version, and a context:\n\nreq = HTTP.Request(\"POST\", \"/widgets\"; headers = [\"Content-Type\" => \"text/plain\"], body = \"hello\")\n\nreq.headers and resp.headers are HTTP.Headers, an ordered vector of String => String pairs; in 1.x they were a Vector of SubString pairs. Header names keep the spelling you give them, and lookups such as HTTP.header(resp, \"content-type\") ignore case. Headers received from the network use Content-Type spelling, while 1.x kept the spelling from the wire. Pass canonicalize_headers = true to send every request header name in that form, as in 1.x. HTTP/2, which 2.0 uses for HTTPS when the server supports it, always sends names in lowercase; pass protocol = :h1 to a server that needs exact spelling. Places that take headers still accept vectors of pairs.","title":"Requests, Responses, and Headers"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Request-Context","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"req.context is still a Dict{Symbol,Any} for application data:\n\nreq.context[:request_id] = request_id\nrequest_id = get(req.context, :request_id, nothing)\n\nCancellation and deadlines live on the HTTP.RequestContext returned by HTTP.get_request_context(req):\n\nctx = HTTP.get_request_context(req)\nHTTP.set_deadline!(ctx, time_ns() + 5_000_000_000)\nHTTP.canceled(ctx) && throw(HTTP.CanceledError(\"request canceled\"))","title":"Request Context"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Clients,-Proxies,-and-TLS","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The pool keyword is ignored. Create a long-lived HTTP.Client instead; it keeps connections, cookies, and settings across requests:\n\nclient = HTTP.Client(transport = HTTP.Transport(max_idle_per_host = 4))\ntry\n    resp = HTTP.get(url; client = client)\nfinally\n    close(client)\nend\n\nAs in 1.x, requests without a client use proxies from the HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, and NO_PROXY environment variables. 2.0 reads them once, when the first such request runs, instead of on every request. The proxy keyword accepts a proxy URL or an HTTP.ProxyConfig, and socks5:// and socks5h:// proxies are supported:\n\nHTTP.get(url; proxy = HTTP.ProxyURL(\"http://proxy.internal:8080\"; no_proxy = \"localhost\"))\nHTTP.get(url; proxy = HTTP.ProxyURL(\"socks5h://proxy.internal:1080\"))\nHTTP.get(url; proxy = nothing)  # connect directly\n\nThe sslconfig and socket_type_tls keywords are ignored. Configure TLS with a Reseau.TLS.Config on the Transport:\n\nusing Reseau\n\nclient = HTTP.Client(transport = HTTP.Transport(tls_config = Reseau.TLS.Config(ca_file = \"ca.pem\")))\n\nrequire_ssl_verification = false still turns off certificate checks for one call.\n\nHTTP.jl 2 does not read the JULIA_SSL_NO_VERIFY_HOSTS and JULIA_NO_VERIFY_HOSTS environment variables: hosts listed there are verified like any other. Pass require_ssl_verification = false, or give their client a Transport with Reseau.TLS.Config(verify_peer = false).","title":"Clients, Proxies, and TLS"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Retries","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The retry_delays and retry_check keywords are ignored. A retry_if function replaces retry_check. Return true to retry and nothing to fall back to the default rules:\n\nBefore:\n\nresp = HTTP.get(url; retries = 3, retry_delays = [0.1, 0.5, 1.0],\n    retry_check = (s, ex, req, resp, resp_body) -> resp !== nothing && resp.status == 409)\n\nAfter:\n\nresp = HTTP.get(url; retries = 3, retry_if = (attempt, err, req, resp) ->\n    resp !== nothing && resp.status == 409 ? true : nothing)\n\nThere is no list of delays. Retries wait a random, exponentially growing delay; pass retry_bucket = HTTP.RetryBucket(backoff_scale_factor_ms = 100) to change its scale. 2.0 retries 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, and 504 responses by default, where 1.x also retried 403, 409, and every other 5xx status. See Retries in the client guide for the full default policy.","title":"Retries"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Timeouts","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"readtimeout still works but is deprecated. It sets read_idle_timeout, the longest wait between reads. Pick the timeout you mean:\n\nresp = HTTP.get(url; connect_timeout = 5, request_timeout = 60, read_idle_timeout = 30)\n\nTimeouts in the client guide describes each keyword. A timeout throws HTTP.TimeoutError, also available as HTTP.HTTPTimeoutError.","title":"Timeouts"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Exceptions","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The exception types live at the top level: HTTP.StatusError, HTTP.ConnectError, HTTP.TimeoutError, and their supertype HTTP.HTTPError. The 1.x names HTTP.Exceptions.StatusError, ConnectError, TimeoutError, and HTTPError still resolve to them, with a deprecation warning under --depwarn=yes. @try and current_exceptions_to_string are gone.\n\nHTTP.RequestError is gone. Failures throw the underlying exception instead, and HTTP.isrecoverable tells you whether a failure is worth retrying:\n\ntry\n    HTTP.get(url)\ncatch e\n    HTTP.isrecoverable(e) || rethrow()\n    # a transient network failure; retry later\nend","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#HTTP.@client","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"HTTP.@client accepts one middleware or a tuple of them in each position. The first middleware in a tuple is the outermost:\n\nHTTP.@client request_middleware\nHTTP.@client request_middleware stream_middleware\nHTTP.@client (outer_request, inner_request) (outer_stream, inner_stream)\n\nRequest middleware wraps request and the verb helpers. Stream middleware wraps open.","title":"HTTP.@client"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Servers","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"HTTP.serve!, HTTP.serve, HTTP.listen!, HTTP.listen, and HTTP.Router keep their 1.x shapes. Some details changed:\n\nServer tasks run on Julia's :interactive thread pool. Start Julia with at least one interactive thread; see the server guide.\nIn a serve! handler, req.body is a body object rather than a Vector{UInt8}. Use String(req.body) to read it as text.\nHTTP.peeraddr(stream) replaces Sockets.getpeername(stream). It returns the client address, or nothing; string(addr) gives \"ip:port\".\nThe server readtimeout keyword still works and sets read_timeout. Each server timeout has a seconds keyword (read_timeout) and a nanoseconds keyword (read_timeout_ns).\n\nBefore:\n\nHTTP.listen(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do stream\n    ip, port = Sockets.getpeername(stream)\nend\n\nAfter:\n\nHTTP.listen(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do stream\n    addr = HTTP.peeraddr(stream)\nend","title":"Servers"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#WebSockets","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The HTTP.WebSockets API keeps its 1.x shape: open, listen!, listen, upgrade, isupgrade, send, receive, and iteration over a socket. Two differences:\n\nmaxframesize defaults to 16 MiB instead of no limit. Pass a larger value if your messages are bigger.\nHTTP.WebSockets.upgrade works only on HTTP/1.1 connections.","title":"WebSockets"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Server-Sent-Events","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"Clients still use the sse_callback keyword:\n\nevents = HTTP.SSEEvent[]\nHTTP.request(\"GET\", url; sse_callback = event -> push!(events, event))\n\nServers return the response that HTTP.sse_stream builds and write events in its do block:\n\nHTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 8080) do req\n    return HTTP.sse_stream(200) do stream\n        write(stream, HTTP.SSEEvent(\"ready\"; event = \"status\", id = \"1\"))\n    end\nend","title":"Server-Sent Events"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Ignored-1.x-Keywords","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The client accepts these 1.x keywords so old calls do not fail, but they have no effect and log a warning: pool, retry_delays, retry_check, sslconfig, socket_type_tls, detect_content_type, observelayers, logerrors, and logtag. readtimeout sets read_idle_timeout, and canonicalize_headers keeps its 1.x meaning.","title":"Ignored 1.x Keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/migration-1x/#Internal-APIs","page":"Migration from 1.x","text":"The 1.x layer stack, connection pool, parser, and socket/TLS extension points have no 2.0 equivalent. Move that code to Client, Transport, Stream, the server functions, Router, HTTP.WebSockets, or the SSE functions. If a use case cannot be expressed with the public API, open an issue that describes it instead of depending on 2.0 internals.","title":"Internal APIs"},{"category":"section","location":"#HTTP.jl","page":"Home","text":"HTTP.jl is an HTTP client and server library for Julia. It speaks HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2, streams request and response bodies, and supports WebSockets and server-sent events. Reseau provides the TCP, DNS, and TLS layers.","title":"HTTP.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"Home","text":"Start a server, send it a request, and read the response body:\n\nusing HTTP\n\nserver = HTTP.serve!(\"127.0.0.1\", 0) do req\n    return HTTP.Response(200; body = \"hello from HTTP.jl\")\nend\n\nresp = HTTP.get(\"http://127.0.0.1:$(HTTP.port(server))/hello\")\nHTTP.forceclose(server)\nString(resp.body)  # \"hello from HTTP.jl\"\n\nPort 0 asks the operating system for a free port; HTTP.port(server) returns the one it chose.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-Go-Next","page":"Home","text":"The Client guide covers requests, streaming, reusable clients, request bodies, retries, and timeouts.\nThe Server guide covers request and stream handlers, routing, static files, server-sent events, and server timeouts.\nThe Protocols guide covers WebSockets and HTTP/2.\nThe Migration guide explains how to update code written for HTTP.jl 1.x.\nThe API reference lists every public name with its docstring.","title":"Where to Go Next"},{"category":"section","location":"#Docs-for-LLMs","page":"Home","text":"The documentation is also published as plain Markdown for LLM tools, following the llms.txt convention:\n\nllms.txt: a short index of the pages.\nllms-full.txt: every page, with all docstrings expanded, in one file.\n\nGive either file to an assistant when you ask it about HTTP.jl.","title":"Docs for LLMs"}]
}
